1
00:00:03,368 --> 00:00:04,609
[SPEAKER_01]: I broke into Martinsburg.

2
00:00:05,429 --> 00:00:08,371
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you don't remember him.

3
00:00:08,411 --> 00:00:17,577
[SPEAKER_01]: He was in Bill Alden's place at the base one time from Cincinnati Reds.

4
00:00:19,198 --> 00:00:20,239
[SPEAKER_01]: That's back to way back.

5
00:00:21,019 --> 00:00:21,860
[SPEAKER_01]: About 1920 was that?

6
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:25,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, hell, it was 1920 when I was in Martinsburg, yes.

7
00:00:25,782 --> 00:00:30,205
[SPEAKER_01]: He lived up there, coached me, and took me to Martinsburg.

8
00:00:30,265 --> 00:00:31,406
[SPEAKER_02]: You were in Lycoming, were you?

9
00:00:31,914 --> 00:00:33,194
[SPEAKER_01]: No, lacos.

10
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:34,495
[SPEAKER_01]: They kept it off.

11
00:00:34,655 --> 00:00:35,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Lonicos.

12
00:00:36,215 --> 00:00:36,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Lonicos?

13
00:00:36,936 --> 00:00:43,738
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, L-O-N-A-C-O-N-I-N-G. And you were pitching sandlot ball there when they saw you?

14
00:00:43,918 --> 00:00:44,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

15
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:45,818
[SPEAKER_02]: This fellow, Loudins?

16
00:00:46,178 --> 00:00:46,879
[SPEAKER_02]: Bill Loudins.

17
00:00:46,959 --> 00:00:47,739
[SPEAKER_02]: Loudins saw you there.

18
00:00:48,179 --> 00:00:50,160
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, was he connected with any organization?

19
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,501
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he wasn't in the Managed Martinsburg Club.

20
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:54,181
[SPEAKER_02]: He took me with him.

21
00:00:54,481 --> 00:00:55,121
[SPEAKER_01]: I see, yeah.

22
00:00:55,141 --> 00:00:56,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Martinsburg, West Virginia.

23
00:00:56,902 --> 00:00:57,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Blue Ridge League.

24
00:00:58,342 --> 00:00:59,122
[SPEAKER_01]: How'd you do there?

25
00:01:00,003 --> 00:01:00,883
[SPEAKER_01]: I wasn't there very long.

26
00:01:02,251 --> 00:01:03,052
[SPEAKER_02]: I went to Baltimore.

27
00:01:04,473 --> 00:01:07,014
[SPEAKER_01]: Jack Dunn's son scouted me a lot up there.

28
00:01:07,575 --> 00:01:09,616
[SPEAKER_02]: Jack Dunn's son saw you in Martinsburg?

29
00:01:09,936 --> 00:01:10,637
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

30
00:01:10,777 --> 00:01:13,639
[SPEAKER_02]: I only pitched six games up in Marble Ridge.

31
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:18,362
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it quite a thrill for you to be playing professional baseball there?

32
00:01:18,422 --> 00:01:19,102
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I didn't know.

33
00:01:19,122 --> 00:01:28,188
[SPEAKER_02]: I said, it's true, it's been a bit of a stretch since I've been home, but... Yeah, I went to Baltimore early.

34
00:01:28,228 --> 00:01:30,630
[SPEAKER_02]: What did your parents think about you going to Pro Bowl?

35
00:01:31,058 --> 00:01:32,178
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't think that about it.

36
00:01:32,619 --> 00:01:33,539
[SPEAKER_02]: What did your father do?

37
00:01:34,159 --> 00:01:35,000
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a coal miner.

38
00:01:35,480 --> 00:01:37,821
[SPEAKER_02]: I see.

39
00:01:38,901 --> 00:01:41,002
[SPEAKER_02]: Was he pretty pleased you'd be going into the bar?

40
00:01:41,322 --> 00:01:43,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I guess.

41
00:01:43,703 --> 00:01:52,107
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't get back home until after the season before the ball went on.

42
00:01:53,608 --> 00:01:55,108
[SPEAKER_00]: I've got your record here.

43
00:01:56,289 --> 00:01:57,029
[SPEAKER_02]: You know this book.

44
00:01:57,529 --> 00:01:58,970
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

45
00:02:00,358 --> 00:02:01,479
[SPEAKER_02]: One thing's puzzled me.

46
00:02:02,820 --> 00:02:06,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Why, when you had such a fine record at Baltimore, you won over 100 ballgames there.

47
00:02:07,003 --> 00:02:07,843
[SPEAKER_02]: 111, was it?

48
00:02:08,083 --> 00:02:08,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

49
00:02:08,524 --> 00:02:09,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, 108, 108.

50
00:02:10,345 --> 00:02:13,827
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a pretty good number, over 100.

51
00:02:14,288 --> 00:02:17,009
[SPEAKER_02]: Why weren't you up in the big time in the games?

52
00:02:17,029 --> 00:02:21,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, them days, there was no drafting in there internationally.

53
00:02:22,013 --> 00:02:22,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Couldn't draft us out.

54
00:02:24,094 --> 00:02:24,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Couldn't draft you.

55
00:02:24,735 --> 00:02:27,457
[SPEAKER_01]: When we'd win pennants there, we'd have to make more money in Baltimore.

56
00:02:28,895 --> 00:02:33,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Now for different ball clubs in the big leagues, we didn't get paid well in Baltimore for minor league club.

57
00:02:33,996 --> 00:02:36,396
[SPEAKER_01]: There was one seven straight penalty there.

58
00:02:36,516 --> 00:02:36,796
[SPEAKER_02]: I know.

59
00:02:37,516 --> 00:02:40,197
[SPEAKER_02]: You think that Baltimore team could have played in the big league?

60
00:02:40,317 --> 00:02:44,478
[SPEAKER_01]: In those days, yes, because we whipped every big league club from down there.

61
00:02:44,518 --> 00:02:47,278
[SPEAKER_01]: We had a ballpark that couldn't put a football field in in Baltimore.

62
00:02:48,958 --> 00:02:52,719
[SPEAKER_01]: We never had no end zones or no side to anything.

63
00:02:53,199 --> 00:02:54,019
[SPEAKER_01]: It was that small.

64
00:02:55,620 --> 00:02:57,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Pretty tough to pitch in a small park.

65
00:02:57,340 --> 00:02:58,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we pitched in it.

66
00:02:59,188 --> 00:03:02,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Earnshaw, Walter, and Tommy Thomas, Jack Onglin.

67
00:03:04,373 --> 00:03:09,417
[SPEAKER_02]: Didn't you kind of feel unhappy that you weren't going up to the Big League?

68
00:03:09,497 --> 00:03:10,178
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

69
00:03:10,218 --> 00:03:13,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Why should we feel unhappy when we get paid this much salary?

70
00:03:14,541 --> 00:03:18,184
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, there's certain glory and fame in the big time.

71
00:03:18,245 --> 00:03:19,065
[SPEAKER_01]: We ain't crazy.

72
00:03:19,085 --> 00:03:21,167
[SPEAKER_01]: We wasn't like that in them days.

73
00:03:22,288 --> 00:03:23,969
[SPEAKER_01]: We wanted to be up at the Big League, sure.

74
00:03:24,370 --> 00:03:25,651
[SPEAKER_01]: We used to go to Washington.

75
00:03:26,428 --> 00:03:29,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Washington playing Washington in our off days.

76
00:03:30,571 --> 00:03:36,054
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was only 40 miles away from Baltimore.

77
00:03:36,755 --> 00:03:38,316
[SPEAKER_01]: How was Jack Dunn to play for?

78
00:03:38,376 --> 00:03:40,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Great.

79
00:03:41,278 --> 00:03:42,919
[SPEAKER_01]: We had no rules on the ballpark.

80
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:45,241
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it was great.

81
00:03:45,501 --> 00:03:49,844
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't have no certain time to be in a night or anything like that.

82
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:53,060
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as you walked out in the field and gave them what you had.

83
00:03:54,181 --> 00:03:56,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So it must have worked out pretty good.

84
00:03:57,401 --> 00:03:58,641
[SPEAKER_01]: We won seven pennants.

85
00:04:00,742 --> 00:04:01,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes sir.

86
00:04:01,802 --> 00:04:03,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they have a Little World Series then?

87
00:04:03,982 --> 00:04:05,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we started in 20.

88
00:04:05,843 --> 00:04:07,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the first Little World Series.

89
00:04:07,483 --> 00:04:08,423
[SPEAKER_02]: How'd you do in that?

90
00:04:08,463 --> 00:04:09,524
[SPEAKER_01]: We beat them.

91
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:10,624
[SPEAKER_01]: We beat St. Paul.

92
00:04:10,744 --> 00:04:12,864
[SPEAKER_01]: We beat St. Paul in that.

93
00:04:13,164 --> 00:04:15,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Who else was on that Baltimore team?

94
00:04:15,705 --> 00:04:17,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Jack Bentley played first base.

95
00:04:17,887 --> 00:04:21,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Bishop played second, Boley played short, Fritz Schmaderer played third.

96
00:04:21,268 --> 00:04:22,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Who was the third baseman?

97
00:04:22,248 --> 00:04:22,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Mazel?

98
00:04:22,929 --> 00:04:23,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Fritz Schmaderer.

99
00:04:23,569 --> 00:04:26,049
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the old third baseman of the Yankees.

100
00:04:27,470 --> 00:04:35,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Bill Holden, Otis Lowry, Merwin Jacobson in the outfield, Ben Higgins, and Dennis Stiles Kessler.

101
00:04:36,653 --> 00:04:38,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Dennis Stiles was up in Philadelphia.

102
00:04:38,473 --> 00:04:41,014
[SPEAKER_01]: And Wahlberg and Earnshaw, you were all together.

103
00:04:41,054 --> 00:04:41,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Wahlberg wasn't there.

104
00:04:42,234 --> 00:04:44,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Earnshaw, Jack Ogden,

105
00:04:45,673 --> 00:04:49,696
[SPEAKER_01]: And Alfonso Thomas, he's a pitcher for the White Sox.

106
00:04:49,796 --> 00:04:51,677
[SPEAKER_02]: A fellow by the name of Harry Frank.

107
00:04:52,858 --> 00:04:53,999
[SPEAKER_02]: A fellow by the name of Clark.

108
00:04:54,659 --> 00:04:57,201
[SPEAKER_02]: How did Dunn get such a good team together?

109
00:04:57,522 --> 00:05:00,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he just got together.

110
00:05:00,384 --> 00:05:02,705
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't tell you that.

111
00:05:03,106 --> 00:05:06,888
[SPEAKER_01]: But he bought me from Martin for, you know, $3,500.

112
00:05:09,339 --> 00:05:11,840
[SPEAKER_02]: You say you fellas were getting better pay than some of the big leaguers?

113
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:15,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we were getting more money down there than the rent shops were getting.

114
00:05:16,461 --> 00:05:17,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

115
00:05:17,522 --> 00:05:19,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we were making $700 a month.

116
00:05:19,562 --> 00:05:20,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

117
00:05:20,642 --> 00:05:20,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

118
00:05:21,543 --> 00:05:23,123
[SPEAKER_02]: How about the tenants?

119
00:05:23,143 --> 00:05:24,224
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you drawing?

120
00:05:24,284 --> 00:05:26,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we were drawing there.

121
00:05:26,904 --> 00:05:29,085
[SPEAKER_01]: We filled a little ballpark up over there.

122
00:05:29,185 --> 00:05:29,665
[SPEAKER_01]: How big?

123
00:05:29,785 --> 00:05:32,546
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't very big, about 8 or 9,000.

124
00:05:32,566 --> 00:05:33,126
[SPEAKER_01]: 8 or 9,000?

125
00:05:33,166 --> 00:05:33,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

126
00:05:33,787 --> 00:05:35,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It was an old wooden ballpark.

127
00:05:35,127 --> 00:05:36,287
[SPEAKER_01]: It burned down pretty late.

128
00:05:37,088 --> 00:05:38,548
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it the site of the...

129
00:05:39,658 --> 00:05:47,864
[SPEAKER_01]: It was on 29th Street and the stadium was on 33rd Street because I lived up past the stadium.

130
00:05:47,884 --> 00:05:54,328
[SPEAKER_01]: I used to have to drive by it every time I went home.

131
00:05:54,468 --> 00:05:55,869
[SPEAKER_01]: East 33rd Street.

132
00:05:58,171 --> 00:05:59,472
[SPEAKER_01]: We all went to the ballpark then.

133
00:06:00,233 --> 00:06:01,553
[SPEAKER_01]: The football stadium.

134
00:06:03,158 --> 00:06:06,020
[SPEAKER_02]: So it was a different field you played on then?

135
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:08,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we had our own ballpark.

136
00:06:08,962 --> 00:06:14,666
[SPEAKER_01]: It was laid out just for football.

137
00:06:14,706 --> 00:06:22,771
[SPEAKER_01]: The ball club took the St. Louis Browns franchise and then they moved everything around to build it different.

138
00:06:23,051 --> 00:06:23,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me just check this one.

139
00:06:23,872 --> 00:06:25,113
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll make sure we're coming in here.

140
00:06:26,193 --> 00:06:27,074
[SPEAKER_02]: I missed that part of it.

141
00:06:28,899 --> 00:06:31,780
[SPEAKER_02]: Bentley was sold to the Giants for $75,000.

142
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,041
[SPEAKER_02]: The Grawl wanted you too.

143
00:06:33,161 --> 00:06:34,601
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, for $75,000.

144
00:06:34,661 --> 00:06:36,482
[SPEAKER_02]: And Dunn wouldn't sell me for $75,000.

145
00:06:36,502 --> 00:06:38,863
[SPEAKER_02]: So Mr. Mack offered him $100,000, $600,000 for me.

146
00:06:38,883 --> 00:06:41,624
[SPEAKER_02]: So that's the reason I went back.

147
00:06:42,284 --> 00:06:44,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Did Dunn make some decision to break the club up?

148
00:06:46,223 --> 00:06:49,904
[SPEAKER_02]: He seemed to start getting rid of everybody about the same time.

149
00:06:49,924 --> 00:06:51,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he lost his son.

150
00:06:51,265 --> 00:06:53,686
[SPEAKER_01]: His son died.

151
00:06:54,046 --> 00:06:56,626
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't take much interest in the ball club after that.

152
00:06:57,687 --> 00:07:00,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So he just left the work and sold to Bishop.

153
00:07:00,608 --> 00:07:04,949
[SPEAKER_01]: When Bishop came to Philadelphia, Bullard came to Philadelphia with him.

154
00:07:05,189 --> 00:07:06,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they go up about the same time you did?

155
00:07:07,030 --> 00:07:07,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Bishop did.

156
00:07:07,610 --> 00:07:08,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Bishop and I were together.

157
00:07:10,311 --> 00:07:13,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they sent a couple of players from Philadelphia down with them.

158
00:07:15,485 --> 00:07:16,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Bishop was in the deal.

159
00:07:20,668 --> 00:07:22,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we had a great ball club down there.

160
00:07:23,009 --> 00:07:26,952
[SPEAKER_02]: There must have been a lot of other clubs after you though, weren't there?

161
00:07:26,972 --> 00:07:30,134
[SPEAKER_02]: Was that record you had of all of them?

162
00:07:30,554 --> 00:07:32,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Other Major League teams that wanted to get you?

163
00:07:32,735 --> 00:07:37,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yeah, I guess it was, but I never know.

164
00:07:37,898 --> 00:07:39,619
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that McGraw won them.

165
00:07:39,639 --> 00:07:43,341
[SPEAKER_01]: McGraw and I would have never got along together.

166
00:07:46,195 --> 00:07:47,596
[SPEAKER_02]: How was Connie Mack to play for?

167
00:07:47,656 --> 00:07:49,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, he was the greatest man in the world.

168
00:07:49,137 --> 00:07:50,778
[SPEAKER_02]: How did he handle his team?

169
00:07:50,838 --> 00:07:55,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he handled...everybody at this time was his own son.

170
00:07:57,681 --> 00:08:00,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was a great fella, a great man.

171
00:08:00,843 --> 00:08:05,206
[SPEAKER_01]: When the old man Mack quit and passed away, I sold them all away.

172
00:08:05,226 --> 00:08:06,787
[SPEAKER_01]: There ain't another guy like him.

173
00:08:07,967 --> 00:08:10,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever have any arguments or disputes with him?

174
00:08:10,108 --> 00:08:11,669
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I never had any arguments with Connie.

175
00:08:16,172 --> 00:08:18,033
[SPEAKER_02]: No, we got along very well.

176
00:08:18,613 --> 00:08:22,555
[SPEAKER_02]: In the early days, sometimes I saw your name spelled with an S on the end of it.

177
00:08:22,755 --> 00:08:24,255
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, well, that didn't make no difference to me.

178
00:08:24,455 --> 00:08:25,776
[SPEAKER_01]: Was that just a mistake?

179
00:08:25,796 --> 00:08:27,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I didn't do it as a mistake.

180
00:08:27,217 --> 00:08:28,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the way they spelled it, I guess.

181
00:08:28,517 --> 00:08:29,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all it was, too.

182
00:08:29,878 --> 00:08:31,338
[SPEAKER_01]: That didn't make the pitch any better.

183
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:39,742
[SPEAKER_02]: I just was confused whether it was really spelled with an S. No, it was D-R-O-V-E is the way it's supposed to be spelled.

184
00:08:39,762 --> 00:08:40,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Uh-huh.

185
00:08:42,603 --> 00:08:44,664
[SPEAKER_02]: Did they call you Lefty down in the minor leagues?

186
00:08:46,196 --> 00:08:48,738
[SPEAKER_02]: How about high school baseball?

187
00:08:48,798 --> 00:08:49,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Never had none.

188
00:08:49,918 --> 00:08:51,099
[SPEAKER_01]: No high school baseball.

189
00:08:51,199 --> 00:08:51,759
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

190
00:08:51,779 --> 00:08:54,821
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you get a chance to pitch much in your teens?

191
00:08:55,381 --> 00:09:05,707
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we played a couple of days a week.

192
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:10,369
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't play from my hometown.

193
00:09:10,389 --> 00:09:11,770
[SPEAKER_01]: They didn't have no clubs.

194
00:09:12,110 --> 00:09:13,811
[SPEAKER_01]: I played from Midland, three miles away.

195
00:09:15,897 --> 00:09:19,601
[SPEAKER_01]: three miles up the road, ride the streetcar, and walk it.

196
00:09:20,301 --> 00:09:21,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, walk it.

197
00:09:24,625 --> 00:09:27,007
[SPEAKER_02]: Was it a pretty big thrill to be with the athletics?

198
00:09:27,328 --> 00:09:29,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you pretty excited to go up the athletics?

199
00:09:29,750 --> 00:09:31,352
[SPEAKER_02]: Or did you just take it in stride?

200
00:09:31,372 --> 00:09:32,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Just took it right in stride.

201
00:09:35,656 --> 00:09:37,858
[SPEAKER_02]: Who were some of the toughest hitters you had now?

202
00:09:37,978 --> 00:09:38,879
[SPEAKER_02]: Say the Yankees of 27 now.

203
00:09:42,458 --> 00:09:44,400
[SPEAKER_01]: They had some good hitters, great hitters.

204
00:09:44,440 --> 00:09:46,341
[SPEAKER_01]: They had some good hitters, they had great hitters.

205
00:09:47,442 --> 00:09:51,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody who walked in that home plate with a bat was tough, if he swung it.

206
00:09:53,908 --> 00:09:54,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Toughest guy of them all.

207
00:09:54,869 --> 00:10:00,113
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, for, you know, you could give a hit to Joe Soule in Cleveland.

208
00:10:01,835 --> 00:10:03,496
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't strike out much.

209
00:10:03,536 --> 00:10:04,957
[SPEAKER_01]: He only struck out one year.

210
00:10:04,997 --> 00:10:06,599
[SPEAKER_01]: He struck out three times in one year.

211
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:08,961
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's how tough he was.

212
00:10:09,001 --> 00:10:10,262
[SPEAKER_01]: He always got in the battle in the ball.

213
00:10:12,262 --> 00:10:12,902
[SPEAKER_02]: How was Ruth?

214
00:10:13,043 --> 00:10:16,665
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't have no trouble with Ruth.

215
00:10:17,065 --> 00:10:24,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I beat every ball club out there more games than they beat me.

216
00:10:24,191 --> 00:10:27,133
[SPEAKER_01]: I was ahead of them all.

217
00:10:27,153 --> 00:10:28,093
[SPEAKER_01]: We had good ballplayers.

218
00:10:28,113 --> 00:10:30,115
[SPEAKER_01]: We had pretty good hitters up there.

219
00:10:31,356 --> 00:10:39,902
[SPEAKER_01]: You take the Yankees, you had Ruth, Garrick, Bob Musil, Earl Coombs, and Bill Dickey.

220
00:10:41,557 --> 00:10:47,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Boston only had one hitter that was a center fielder that had Flagstead.

221
00:10:47,942 --> 00:10:53,907
[SPEAKER_01]: St. Louis only had two real hitters, a fellow named Williams and a fellow by the name of George Sisler.

222
00:10:54,227 --> 00:10:55,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you pitch against Sisler?

223
00:10:55,528 --> 00:10:56,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh yes.

224
00:10:56,329 --> 00:10:56,809
[SPEAKER_01]: How was he?

225
00:10:57,429 --> 00:11:00,191
[SPEAKER_01]: I said he was a good hitter.

226
00:11:00,832 --> 00:11:03,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Caldwell had Mossel and Bibb Falk.

227
00:11:07,712 --> 00:11:11,376
[SPEAKER_01]: They were pretty good hitters.

228
00:11:11,416 --> 00:11:14,078
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Cleveland?

229
00:11:14,438 --> 00:11:15,399
[SPEAKER_00]: My favorite team.

230
00:11:16,600 --> 00:11:18,302
[SPEAKER_01]: I forget who they were.

231
00:11:19,142 --> 00:11:22,606
[SPEAKER_01]: They had Boswick and Trotsky.

232
00:11:22,766 --> 00:11:23,987
[SPEAKER_01]: They had Dick Porter over there.

233
00:11:33,191 --> 00:11:35,353
[SPEAKER_02]: I saw you beat them a number of times out there.

234
00:11:35,393 --> 00:11:38,435
[SPEAKER_02]: They didn't give you much trouble as I recall.

235
00:11:38,755 --> 00:11:40,016
[SPEAKER_02]: That little ballpark too.

236
00:11:40,937 --> 00:11:45,860
[SPEAKER_02]: How was it pitching where you had a short fence like League Park or Yankee Stadium?

237
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:47,461
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you just pitch away all the time.

238
00:11:47,622 --> 00:11:49,723
[SPEAKER_01]: You just pitch away, huh?

239
00:11:49,743 --> 00:11:49,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

240
00:11:51,144 --> 00:11:55,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you pitch like you're a left-hand hitter so you just pitch it outside all the time.

241
00:11:56,308 --> 00:11:58,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you have any favorite parks you liked to pitch in at all?

242
00:11:58,749 --> 00:12:00,210
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I didn't.

243
00:12:01,367 --> 00:12:05,810
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess Chicago was the best ballpark and mall to pitch in.

244
00:12:05,910 --> 00:12:07,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It was bigger than it is now.

245
00:12:07,991 --> 00:12:11,593
[SPEAKER_01]: They fenced the center field out of Chicago now.

246
00:12:12,533 --> 00:12:17,156
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't have that.

247
00:12:17,176 --> 00:12:19,097
[SPEAKER_01]: The Yankees was a good ballpark to pitch in.

248
00:12:21,538 --> 00:12:23,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Boston was a short left field fence.

249
00:12:25,300 --> 00:12:30,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, our ballpark in Philadelphia was only about 330 feet down each foul line.

250
00:12:31,326 --> 00:12:33,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Did they have a high fence at first?

251
00:12:33,607 --> 00:12:38,348
[SPEAKER_01]: No, the fence, they had a concrete fence about 12 feet high at first.

252
00:12:39,388 --> 00:12:43,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they built a big steel fence.

253
00:12:43,210 --> 00:12:46,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that was after I even quit there.

254
00:12:47,291 --> 00:12:52,072
[SPEAKER_01]: No, after I left there, they put a new fence up on top of the steel.

255
00:12:53,493 --> 00:12:57,114
[SPEAKER_01]: So the people on them houses across the street couldn't see in.

256
00:12:58,834 --> 00:13:00,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, heck, we only could get in about a block.

257
00:13:01,650 --> 00:13:03,870
[SPEAKER_01]: thirty-two, thirty-three thousand and put us out there.

258
00:13:04,551 --> 00:13:05,351
[SPEAKER_02]: I never was in a game.

259
00:13:05,371 --> 00:13:09,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, he kind of broke up the ball club because he, I mean, he could have drawn up money.

260
00:13:10,052 --> 00:13:15,533
[SPEAKER_01]: People didn't, well, we'd draw good, but he was paying big salaries, and they just couldn't.

261
00:13:15,573 --> 00:13:16,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, he had to break them up.

262
00:13:17,493 --> 00:13:19,133
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a tremendous team.

263
00:13:19,173 --> 00:13:19,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Boy.

264
00:13:19,894 --> 00:13:22,094
[SPEAKER_00]: He really was hard-pressed for funds.

265
00:13:22,114 --> 00:13:23,774
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he broke that team up.

266
00:13:23,994 --> 00:13:27,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we won the World Series, we won the Penn, the World Series in twenty-nine.

267
00:13:29,206 --> 00:13:32,128
[SPEAKER_01]: In 31 we lost, I forget how many hundred thousand people.

268
00:13:32,648 --> 00:13:34,110
[SPEAKER_01]: We lost 30.

269
00:13:34,990 --> 00:13:36,051
[SPEAKER_01]: In 31 we lost more.

270
00:13:36,071 --> 00:13:38,473
[SPEAKER_01]: We won the peasant 30.

271
00:13:38,593 --> 00:13:40,574
[SPEAKER_01]: In 31 we still didn't... Lost money?

272
00:13:40,594 --> 00:13:41,295
[SPEAKER_01]: We lost money.

273
00:13:41,455 --> 00:13:42,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

274
00:13:42,295 --> 00:13:44,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course the depression years were pretty bad.

275
00:13:44,617 --> 00:13:46,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it was the depression.

276
00:13:46,738 --> 00:13:49,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Simmons was getting about $33,000 a year in that.

277
00:13:50,141 --> 00:13:51,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Myself and Ernst Shaw.

278
00:13:51,842 --> 00:13:52,823
[SPEAKER_01]: You were getting over $30,000?

279
00:13:52,843 --> 00:13:54,724
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we didn't get that much.

280
00:13:54,744 --> 00:13:56,685
[SPEAKER_01]: Simmons was the highest payer on the mall.

281
00:13:59,349 --> 00:14:03,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have any contract arguments with Connie Meyer?

282
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:03,790
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

283
00:14:05,070 --> 00:14:09,051
[SPEAKER_01]: He sent a contract, he didn't like it, just wrote back telling him he didn't like it, and that was it.

284
00:14:10,571 --> 00:14:12,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Held out one year, not very long.

285
00:14:13,111 --> 00:14:15,392
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he handle all the contracts on you?

286
00:14:15,752 --> 00:14:15,952
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

287
00:14:16,372 --> 00:14:18,672
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a general manager and manager, I guess.

288
00:14:18,692 --> 00:14:23,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Yep, he was everything.

289
00:14:23,693 --> 00:14:25,014
[SPEAKER_02]: He was a great old man.

290
00:14:26,854 --> 00:14:27,494
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, sir.

291
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:31,341
[SPEAKER_02]: Where did you live while you were with the Philadelphia team?

292
00:14:31,622 --> 00:14:33,663
[SPEAKER_02]: In Philadelphia.

293
00:14:33,703 --> 00:14:35,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you own property there?

294
00:14:35,644 --> 00:14:36,104
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

295
00:14:36,844 --> 00:14:38,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I came back home in the wintertime.

296
00:14:38,665 --> 00:14:39,726
[SPEAKER_01]: The last season was over.

297
00:14:40,106 --> 00:14:41,026
[SPEAKER_01]: To Lone Oconee?

298
00:14:41,046 --> 00:14:42,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Yep.

299
00:14:42,387 --> 00:14:43,688
[SPEAKER_02]: What did you do there in the winters?

300
00:14:43,868 --> 00:14:44,208
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing.

301
00:14:44,388 --> 00:14:45,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Hunt fish.

302
00:14:46,629 --> 00:14:49,191
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you own a... Bowl Valley.

303
00:14:49,891 --> 00:14:53,433
[SPEAKER_02]: Was that during your playing days or was that later?

304
00:14:53,473 --> 00:14:54,053
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

305
00:14:54,193 --> 00:14:56,334
[SPEAKER_02]: I built it in there in about 29.

306
00:15:00,651 --> 00:15:02,272
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you married while you were at Baltimore?

307
00:15:02,292 --> 00:15:02,572
[SPEAKER_02]: Yep.

308
00:15:03,272 --> 00:15:04,572
[SPEAKER_02]: When did you get married?

309
00:15:04,592 --> 00:15:08,853
[SPEAKER_02]: 1920, 1921.

310
00:15:09,273 --> 00:15:11,113
[SPEAKER_02]: Was your wife from hometown?

311
00:15:12,014 --> 00:15:12,494
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

312
00:15:12,514 --> 00:15:15,454
[SPEAKER_02]: She only lived a half block from me.

313
00:15:15,554 --> 00:15:16,015
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

314
00:15:16,315 --> 00:15:16,635
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

315
00:15:17,195 --> 00:15:20,115
[SPEAKER_02]: Did she travel with you much?

316
00:15:20,255 --> 00:15:20,976
[SPEAKER_02]: Not on the road.

317
00:15:21,116 --> 00:15:24,897
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I was in spring training and during the World Series she'd go.

318
00:15:27,742 --> 00:15:31,844
[SPEAKER_01]: I took the trip to Japan and she wanted to go with us.

319
00:15:32,444 --> 00:15:37,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Fred Lee handled that, didn't he?

320
00:15:37,786 --> 00:15:38,266
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

321
00:15:38,446 --> 00:15:46,509
[SPEAKER_02]: We have a group of baseball historians and researchers which had a meeting in Philadelphia just about a month ago and Fred Lee

322
00:15:48,243 --> 00:15:49,203
[SPEAKER_02]: He's an old boy.

323
00:15:49,263 --> 00:15:51,264
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, his sister lives in Haverford.

324
00:15:51,304 --> 00:15:51,824
[SPEAKER_02]: He was there.

325
00:15:52,184 --> 00:15:53,044
[SPEAKER_02]: He came to talk to us.

326
00:15:53,124 --> 00:15:53,524
[SPEAKER_02]: He's 86 now.

327
00:15:53,564 --> 00:15:54,685
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, he's pretty old.

328
00:15:54,725 --> 00:15:56,005
[SPEAKER_02]: But he's in good shape.

329
00:15:56,025 --> 00:15:58,586
[SPEAKER_02]: He's a little thin guy in this game.

330
00:15:59,306 --> 00:16:00,186
[SPEAKER_02]: Great baseball writer.

331
00:16:00,246 --> 00:16:00,646
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

332
00:16:00,826 --> 00:16:02,407
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he doesn't know about baseball.

333
00:16:02,427 --> 00:16:05,168
[SPEAKER_01]: He goes, he's up in Cooperstown every year.

334
00:16:06,708 --> 00:16:10,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he knows a lot of baseball.

335
00:16:10,169 --> 00:16:12,129
[SPEAKER_02]: Does your wife like baseball?

336
00:16:12,229 --> 00:16:13,330
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, she does, yeah.

337
00:16:13,430 --> 00:16:14,650
[SPEAKER_02]: She go out to the games all the time?

338
00:16:14,670 --> 00:16:15,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, every day.

339
00:16:15,110 --> 00:16:15,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

340
00:16:15,670 --> 00:16:15,950
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

341
00:16:17,031 --> 00:16:17,891
[SPEAKER_02]: Did she, uh...

342
00:16:18,753 --> 00:16:20,254
[SPEAKER_02]: No, she never says nothing.

343
00:16:20,514 --> 00:16:21,455
[SPEAKER_02]: No, she's just there.

344
00:16:21,975 --> 00:16:23,396
[SPEAKER_02]: You get nervous when you're pitching?

345
00:16:23,416 --> 00:16:23,876
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

346
00:16:23,896 --> 00:16:26,057
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know why she'd need to be.

347
00:16:29,699 --> 00:16:31,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever get knocked out of the box very often?

348
00:16:32,421 --> 00:16:33,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I couldn't.

349
00:16:33,721 --> 00:16:35,622
[SPEAKER_01]: You ask me questions I can't answer.

350
00:16:35,822 --> 00:16:36,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

351
00:16:36,483 --> 00:16:38,904
[SPEAKER_01]: See, I wouldn't know how much or how many times.

352
00:16:38,924 --> 00:16:41,906
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'm just trying to think of how it would be handled by Connie Mack.

353
00:16:41,926 --> 00:16:43,667
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you just send them back.

354
00:16:43,687 --> 00:16:45,108
[SPEAKER_01]: See, Connie never wore a uniform.

355
00:16:46,608 --> 00:16:47,729
[SPEAKER_01]: She sent his coach out.

356
00:16:49,992 --> 00:16:51,332
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a different rule now.

357
00:16:51,372 --> 00:16:56,133
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, back in the day, you'd go up the hill, go up the mountain, off if you wanted.

358
00:16:56,733 --> 00:17:01,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you're going to do it a second time out, you automatically have the pitchers out.

359
00:17:01,914 --> 00:17:04,555
[SPEAKER_01]: We still played the games faster than we did in the day.

360
00:17:05,935 --> 00:17:07,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Most pitchers finish the games, too.

361
00:17:07,835 --> 00:17:09,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, we only had seven or eight pitchers.

362
00:17:09,976 --> 00:17:13,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Now we've got ten or eleven.

363
00:17:15,037 --> 00:17:17,517
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all really pitching now, mostly.

364
00:17:18,268 --> 00:17:28,094
[SPEAKER_01]: They have a lot of good ball players, heck of a lot of good ball players, but they've got different equipment than we had.

365
00:17:28,114 --> 00:17:37,639
[SPEAKER_01]: The guns and everything is a lot different, the fields are different, there are diamonds, there are pool tables, we are a little rough.

366
00:17:39,158 --> 00:17:40,799
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you still have much interest in the game?

367
00:17:40,959 --> 00:17:42,760
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you watch it or read about it?

368
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:49,344
[SPEAKER_01]: I go to Cleveland and when I saw Perry pitch his 14th straight, I think it was his 14th, 14th straight.

369
00:17:49,404 --> 00:17:53,306
[SPEAKER_02]: On a Friday rain, I think.

370
00:17:54,366 --> 00:17:58,488
[SPEAKER_02]: Were you pretty interested in this effort of his to tie your record?

371
00:17:58,508 --> 00:17:59,509
[SPEAKER_01]: I was pulling for him.

372
00:18:00,029 --> 00:18:01,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you hear that game that night?

373
00:18:01,450 --> 00:18:01,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

374
00:18:02,090 --> 00:18:04,332
[SPEAKER_01]: That was a heartbreaker.

375
00:18:04,432 --> 00:18:04,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

376
00:18:06,847 --> 00:18:11,429
[SPEAKER_01]: He pitches at night.

377
00:18:11,449 --> 00:18:12,349
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a great pitcher.

378
00:18:13,170 --> 00:18:16,511
[SPEAKER_02]: What about this pitball business?

379
00:18:16,591 --> 00:18:17,972
[SPEAKER_02]: Was there much of that in your time?

380
00:18:18,512 --> 00:18:20,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we had pitball pitchers in there.

381
00:18:20,493 --> 00:18:22,213
[SPEAKER_02]: Jack Quinn, Ben Faber.

382
00:18:22,413 --> 00:18:25,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Those that pitched before the band, but I mean afterwards.

383
00:18:25,455 --> 00:18:27,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Any illegal pitching?

384
00:18:27,555 --> 00:18:31,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there was no illegal, but some of them thought of it.

385
00:18:36,967 --> 00:18:39,828
[SPEAKER_02]: What was that great year you had, 31?

386
00:18:40,048 --> 00:18:45,189
[SPEAKER_02]: 1931, you won 31, huh?

387
00:18:48,530 --> 00:18:49,731
[SPEAKER_02]: That must have been quite a year.

388
00:18:50,331 --> 00:18:52,911
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you remember much about that year?

389
00:18:52,951 --> 00:18:54,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Not so much, no.

390
00:18:55,012 --> 00:18:58,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Pitching my regular turn every fourth day, three days rest.

391
00:19:01,734 --> 00:19:03,974
[SPEAKER_02]: You led the league, you earned run average, too.

392
00:19:04,014 --> 00:19:05,875
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you lost your run average, so it must have been

393
00:19:08,158 --> 00:19:10,579
[SPEAKER_02]: You had great hitting support, but you had great pitching, too.

394
00:19:11,359 --> 00:19:12,759
[SPEAKER_02]: How about your defense behind you?

395
00:19:12,819 --> 00:19:15,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, we had good, pretty good defense.

396
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:18,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, at first, when I went up, it wasn't so good.

397
00:19:19,501 --> 00:19:25,722
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, I went up in 25. Who was the first baseman then?

398
00:19:25,742 --> 00:19:26,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Fox wasn't up yet, was he?

399
00:19:27,403 --> 00:19:33,624
[SPEAKER_02]: No, Fox comes up as a catcher.

400
00:19:34,945 --> 00:19:35,265
[SPEAKER_01]: J.O.

401
00:19:35,285 --> 00:19:35,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Joe Hauser.

402
00:19:37,461 --> 00:19:38,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he broke his kneecap.

403
00:19:40,143 --> 00:19:43,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Then Bishop was there.

404
00:19:44,767 --> 00:19:45,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Mowley was.

405
00:19:45,307 --> 00:19:49,030
[SPEAKER_01]: We found a guy named Chick Galloway at the shortstop.

406
00:19:50,011 --> 00:19:52,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Jimmy Dykes III, yeah, Jimmy was there.

407
00:19:53,133 --> 00:19:55,715
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the third baseman in Jimmy Dykes.

408
00:19:56,736 --> 00:19:58,798
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a guy I think ought to be in the Hall of Fame.

409
00:20:00,439 --> 00:20:02,741
[SPEAKER_01]: With his record, I mean, he was a record.

410
00:20:08,812 --> 00:20:12,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Bing was an outfielder.

411
00:20:12,317 --> 00:20:15,341
[SPEAKER_01]: Bing Miller and Mule Hatch and Roger Cramer and Al Simmons.

412
00:20:15,381 --> 00:20:17,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But Mule Hatch just died.

413
00:20:17,183 --> 00:20:20,067
[SPEAKER_01]: A couple of weeks ago I know.

414
00:20:27,164 --> 00:20:30,445
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you fellas get together much in your retirement days at all?

415
00:20:30,485 --> 00:20:34,306
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we used to get together once in a while.

416
00:20:34,326 --> 00:20:41,888
[SPEAKER_01]: We'd come and have some of these old-timers fill it up.

417
00:20:41,988 --> 00:20:43,769
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't saw Earnshaw for a long while.

418
00:20:44,489 --> 00:20:46,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Since the last time we filled it up.

419
00:20:46,570 --> 00:20:47,750
[SPEAKER_01]: He's in Arkansas somewhere.

420
00:20:47,790 --> 00:20:50,631
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a great right-hand pitcher.

421
00:20:59,365 --> 00:21:00,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Is Wahlberg still around?

