[SPEAKER_02]: I think they want to control the commissioner, don't you think?

636
00:31:56,411 --> 00:31:57,032
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know.

637
00:31:57,072 --> 00:31:57,912
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't believe that.

638
00:31:58,253 --> 00:31:59,474
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't believe that.

639
00:32:01,615 --> 00:32:02,776
[SPEAKER_01]: It's different altogether.

640
00:32:03,557 --> 00:32:04,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Altogether.

641
00:32:05,944 --> 00:32:06,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything's different.

642
00:32:12,149 --> 00:32:13,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's everything.

643
00:32:13,250 --> 00:32:15,012
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a lot of difference.

644
00:32:15,072 --> 00:32:16,113
[SPEAKER_01]: All parts of difference.

645
00:32:16,613 --> 00:32:19,155
[SPEAKER_01]: It's everything.

646
00:32:19,996 --> 00:32:21,878
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's some great ballplayers up there.

647
00:32:22,098 --> 00:32:28,984
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, they're all young and they look like they can go for a year, quite a few years, you know.

648
00:32:31,327 --> 00:32:33,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you said how you just love to play ball, you know.

649
00:32:33,649 --> 00:32:36,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I used to play ball neat.

650
00:32:37,312 --> 00:32:39,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, man.

651
00:32:39,594 --> 00:32:44,858
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a ballpark every morning, early around 9 o'clock in the morning, and we didn't play until 3 o'clock in the afternoon.

652
00:32:46,340 --> 00:32:48,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Never started a doubleheader at 2 o'clock.

653
00:32:48,241 --> 00:32:49,522
[SPEAKER_01]: We had no lights to turn on.

654
00:32:51,884 --> 00:32:53,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Now they turn the lights on at 3 o'clock in the afternoon.

655
00:32:56,448 --> 00:32:57,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever pitch night ball?

656
00:32:58,030 --> 00:32:59,531
[SPEAKER_01]: Nope, never pitched a game at night.

657
00:33:02,428 --> 00:33:05,210
[SPEAKER_01]: I wonder what effect that had.

658
00:33:05,250 --> 00:33:14,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in my latter couple of years, they had me put in Lake Moline, and Chicago, and Philadelphia, and Cleveland.

659
00:33:18,259 --> 00:33:19,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So they didn't bother.

660
00:33:21,201 --> 00:33:22,182
[SPEAKER_01]: We left with this thing.

661
00:33:22,362 --> 00:33:24,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I wasn't real fast then.

662
00:33:24,203 --> 00:33:26,405
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, it was the tail end of my career.

663
00:33:27,298 --> 00:33:31,559
[SPEAKER_02]: You say your fastball left you, or you weren't as fast?

664
00:33:31,579 --> 00:33:34,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know now, what year.

665
00:33:34,119 --> 00:33:35,220
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't take the year out.

666
00:33:35,260 --> 00:33:36,820
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a gradual process.

667
00:33:38,660 --> 00:33:40,141
[SPEAKER_01]: I had a good curveball then.

668
00:33:40,301 --> 00:33:43,361
[SPEAKER_01]: I developed a good curveball and a good fartball.

669
00:33:44,841 --> 00:33:46,782
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you have a curveball earlier?

670
00:33:47,002 --> 00:33:51,443
[SPEAKER_02]: No, of course, about five or six years after I got there.

671
00:33:53,925 --> 00:33:55,606
[SPEAKER_02]: Did anybody teach you that?

672
00:33:55,666 --> 00:33:57,227
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we had no pitcher coaches.

673
00:33:57,747 --> 00:34:00,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Did other pitchers help you when you come up at all?

674
00:34:00,088 --> 00:34:04,231
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we were on our own.

675
00:34:04,271 --> 00:34:05,491
[SPEAKER_01]: We only had two coaches.

676
00:34:06,472 --> 00:34:06,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Who were they?

677
00:34:07,312 --> 00:34:14,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Eddie Collins and Kit Gleason.

678
00:34:15,357 --> 00:34:17,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Gleason knows baseball pretty well.

679
00:34:17,058 --> 00:34:19,519
[SPEAKER_02]: He's the old Chicago manager.

680
00:34:21,757 --> 00:34:24,579
[SPEAKER_01]: That really broke him up until 1919.

681
00:34:25,240 --> 00:34:26,100
[SPEAKER_01]: He managed that.

682
00:34:26,120 --> 00:34:27,341
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he ever talk much about that?

683
00:34:27,361 --> 00:34:27,661
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

684
00:34:28,482 --> 00:34:31,104
[SPEAKER_01]: We never mentioned it to him.

685
00:34:31,124 --> 00:34:33,386
[SPEAKER_01]: We know how much it hurt him and we never bothered him.

686
00:34:33,426 --> 00:34:35,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, Eddie Collins was on that team too.

687
00:34:35,367 --> 00:34:35,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Collins.

688
00:34:36,008 --> 00:34:37,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Did he ever say much?

689
00:34:37,148 --> 00:34:38,369
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

690
00:34:38,449 --> 00:34:41,191
[SPEAKER_01]: We never asked no questions about it.

691
00:34:41,652 --> 00:34:42,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Too sensitive, wasn't he?

692
00:34:42,592 --> 00:34:42,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Yep.

693
00:34:43,093 --> 00:34:44,634
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't want to bother him.

694
00:34:45,154 --> 00:34:47,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring back memories like that stuff to him.

695
00:34:50,781 --> 00:34:55,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Was there ever much feeling about you fellows about kicking those guys out?

696
00:34:55,965 --> 00:34:56,905
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever think much about it?

697
00:34:56,925 --> 00:34:57,766
[SPEAKER_01]: No, never thought about it.

698
00:34:58,086 --> 00:35:10,615
[SPEAKER_02]: Never thought about it.

699
00:35:11,456 --> 00:35:14,198
[SPEAKER_02]: When you played in Cleveland, where did the team stay?

700
00:35:15,779 --> 00:35:17,540
[SPEAKER_02]: It was the old Holland Hotel.

701
00:35:17,680 --> 00:35:18,000
[SPEAKER_01]: I see.

702
00:35:19,532 --> 00:35:23,994
[SPEAKER_01]: We didn't stay at the Cleveland Hotel until I went to Boston, then they moved to the Cleveland Hotel.

703
00:35:24,074 --> 00:35:26,294
[SPEAKER_02]: That's when they moved down the stadium, huh?

704
00:35:26,334 --> 00:35:30,236
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, well, uh... Yeah, I pitched the first game when I was a little out of it.

705
00:35:30,256 --> 00:35:31,756
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, you pitched against Bill Harder that day.

706
00:35:31,796 --> 00:35:32,897
[SPEAKER_01]: I was at Philadelphia.

707
00:35:33,257 --> 00:35:35,117
[SPEAKER_01]: We used to haul around the hotel there.

708
00:35:36,038 --> 00:35:36,498
[SPEAKER_02]: What was that?

709
00:35:36,558 --> 00:35:38,279
[SPEAKER_02]: That must have been a big thrill, wasn't it?

710
00:35:38,419 --> 00:35:39,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it was.

711
00:35:39,699 --> 00:35:43,680
[SPEAKER_01]: We would pitch the open stadium, just like opening the open game.

712
00:35:43,700 --> 00:35:48,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, a lot of people thought that was a great event.

713
00:35:52,744 --> 00:35:57,209
[SPEAKER_02]: Cleveland's home run output was cut in half when they moved down the stadium.

714
00:35:57,229 --> 00:35:59,250
[SPEAKER_02]: They couldn't hit the ball out of that part.

715
00:35:59,291 --> 00:36:01,493
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we didn't have, there was no fence like you got out there.

716
00:36:01,513 --> 00:36:04,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You hit one between the outfields and drove it over miles.

717
00:36:10,141 --> 00:36:13,884
[SPEAKER_02]: April, I think, had 32 home runs before then, about 10 after that.

718
00:36:14,584 --> 00:36:16,806
[SPEAKER_02]: His triple output went up just the other way.

719
00:36:16,826 --> 00:36:17,567
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, sure.

720
00:36:17,587 --> 00:36:18,828
[SPEAKER_01]: In between the fielder, you know.

721
00:36:18,848 --> 00:36:21,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it rolled about right center and left center.

722
00:36:21,670 --> 00:36:24,852
[SPEAKER_01]: It rolled just like Yankee Stadium was.

723
00:36:26,193 --> 00:36:30,636
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess the Yankees, their stadium would be a different ballpark altogether after you get remodeled.

724
00:36:31,437 --> 00:36:32,858
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess you're tearing everything out of there.

725
00:36:32,898 --> 00:36:32,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Yep.

726
00:36:33,018 --> 00:36:34,659
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

727
00:36:34,879 --> 00:36:35,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it needed?

728
00:36:36,481 --> 00:36:38,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, imagine that it was in there since when, 22?

729
00:36:43,112 --> 00:36:44,873
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't hold as many people, I don't think.

730
00:36:45,293 --> 00:36:47,834
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess not.

731
00:36:49,075 --> 00:36:50,376
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't like these new parks.

732
00:36:50,396 --> 00:36:53,378
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't strike me as baseball parks.

733
00:36:53,398 --> 00:36:57,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, one of these new ones they built, they're a regulated park.

734
00:36:57,960 --> 00:37:02,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Both foul lines are built the same, same space all the way.

735
00:37:03,123 --> 00:37:06,565
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing I don't like about it is that acid turf.

736
00:37:06,605 --> 00:37:08,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Boy, that's hot in the feet.

737
00:37:09,880 --> 00:37:12,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But we were going to St. Louis and that.

738
00:37:12,162 --> 00:37:15,925
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, standing, standing ought to be an introduction.

739
00:37:16,305 --> 00:37:17,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Old-timers, eh?

740
00:37:17,766 --> 00:37:18,506
[SPEAKER_01]: You feel the heat.

741
00:37:18,887 --> 00:37:19,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

742
00:37:19,727 --> 00:37:20,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

743
00:37:21,289 --> 00:37:24,671
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's changed defensive play, too, the way...

744
00:37:24,691 --> 00:37:26,332
[SPEAKER_02]: The ball really takes off on that.

