00:38:01,773 --> 00:38:03,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let's see.

753
00:38:05,405 --> 00:38:06,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Yankee was pretty good.

754
00:38:06,586 --> 00:38:08,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Irish and Boston was good.

755
00:38:09,947 --> 00:38:11,168
[SPEAKER_01]: St. Louis was lousy.

756
00:38:12,088 --> 00:38:13,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Washington was lousy.

757
00:38:13,869 --> 00:38:15,310
[SPEAKER_01]: It was lousy.

758
00:38:16,791 --> 00:38:18,091
[SPEAKER_01]: How about Link Park in Cleveland?

759
00:38:18,491 --> 00:38:19,512
[SPEAKER_01]: It was lousy too.

760
00:38:21,653 --> 00:38:22,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Terrible, terrible.

761
00:38:26,897 --> 00:38:28,838
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever play in Baker Bowl at all?

762
00:38:28,958 --> 00:38:29,578
[SPEAKER_02]: Exhibition games?

763
00:38:29,598 --> 00:38:30,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, exhibition games.

764
00:38:30,599 --> 00:38:33,180
[SPEAKER_01]: We played the Phillies in the spring of the year.

765
00:38:33,200 --> 00:38:35,681
[SPEAKER_01]: That would have been some strange ballpark, wouldn't it?

766
00:38:35,701 --> 00:38:36,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it got us scared to death.

767
00:38:36,701 --> 00:38:37,982
[SPEAKER_01]: We weren't even walking on it.

768
00:38:38,022 --> 00:38:39,983
[SPEAKER_01]: We'd get into the bench.

769
00:38:40,003 --> 00:38:40,743
[SPEAKER_01]: We could go fall in.

770
00:38:40,763 --> 00:38:41,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It was all wood.

771
00:38:41,623 --> 00:38:42,203
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

772
00:38:42,404 --> 00:38:46,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you got them timbered up and underneath and everything.

773
00:38:46,345 --> 00:38:47,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Miracle never fell in.

774
00:38:48,886 --> 00:38:50,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Boy, that was a terrible ballpark.

775
00:38:50,707 --> 00:38:52,408
[SPEAKER_01]: See, we dressed our own ballpark.

776
00:38:52,428 --> 00:38:55,389
[SPEAKER_01]: We had to go down and get in cabs.

777
00:38:56,022 --> 00:38:57,863
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, I see you didn't take a chance down there, huh?

778
00:38:57,883 --> 00:38:59,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you never dressed down there.

779
00:38:59,965 --> 00:39:01,266
[SPEAKER_02]: They did have a dressing room, though.

780
00:39:01,546 --> 00:39:02,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, they did.

781
00:39:02,527 --> 00:39:04,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, they always had a dressing room down there.

782
00:39:04,528 --> 00:39:05,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Centerfield.

783
00:39:05,789 --> 00:39:06,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I see.

784
00:39:06,730 --> 00:39:09,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the Giants used to do in Centerfield.

785
00:39:13,615 --> 00:39:17,378
[SPEAKER_02]: I guess that right field fence in Baker Bowl was kind of close.

786
00:39:17,398 --> 00:39:20,400
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, right fielder, he had the extra second baseman.

787
00:39:21,781 --> 00:39:22,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Boom, boom, back, you know.

788
00:39:23,122 --> 00:39:24,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that tin fence?

789
00:39:28,407 --> 00:39:30,247
[SPEAKER_02]: That's pretty good ballplayers over there, Earl.

790
00:39:30,908 --> 00:39:34,028
[SPEAKER_02]: Did Chuck Kline hit all the home runs at any other park, you think?

791
00:39:34,068 --> 00:39:35,409
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, Chuck was a good hitter.

792
00:39:35,429 --> 00:39:37,849
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a strong hitter.

793
00:39:39,730 --> 00:39:41,170
[SPEAKER_02]: He got a little bit of help there, though.

794
00:39:41,570 --> 00:39:42,270
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sure.

795
00:39:49,232 --> 00:39:49,912
[SPEAKER_02]: Did, uh...

796
00:39:53,435 --> 00:39:56,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Well Dizzy Dean wasn't pitching for the Cardinals in the World Series, was he?

797
00:39:57,397 --> 00:39:58,277
[SPEAKER_02]: No, he wasn't up there.

798
00:39:58,297 --> 00:40:00,278
[SPEAKER_01]: It's 34 and 35, that's against Detroit.

799
00:40:00,298 --> 00:40:01,358
[SPEAKER_01]: He went up in 32, didn't he?

800
00:40:01,378 --> 00:40:02,119
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I think so.

801
00:40:02,139 --> 00:40:04,160
[SPEAKER_02]: I think it was about 32 he went up.

802
00:40:04,340 --> 00:40:06,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you know him very well?

803
00:40:06,100 --> 00:40:09,262
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't diss very well, that's for good.

804
00:40:19,659 --> 00:40:20,840
[SPEAKER_01]: He's sort of backwards.

805
00:40:20,880 --> 00:40:22,481
[SPEAKER_01]: He's crazy as he sounds.

806
00:40:22,641 --> 00:40:24,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he's just as crazy as he was.

807
00:40:24,142 --> 00:40:24,922
[SPEAKER_01]: He's a great guy.

808
00:40:25,062 --> 00:40:28,144
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he's a great guy.

809
00:40:30,065 --> 00:40:31,807
[SPEAKER_01]: I never met his brother, Paul.

810
00:40:31,867 --> 00:40:37,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Had he been ill before his death here, do you know?

811
00:40:37,910 --> 00:40:38,751
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I don't know.

812
00:40:38,831 --> 00:40:40,712
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't hear anything about it.

813
00:40:40,732 --> 00:40:41,352
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't either.

814
00:40:41,753 --> 00:40:43,514
[SPEAKER_01]: He was playing golf with another capo.

815
00:40:46,978 --> 00:40:53,863
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been in a hospital a day or two before, but I guess that was something that just developed there.

816
00:41:01,309 --> 00:41:08,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you recall any really amusing or funny things that happened while you were playing ball?

817
00:41:10,459 --> 00:41:11,880
[SPEAKER_02]: So many things happened, you know.

818
00:41:11,900 --> 00:41:17,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you know, we just knew they were along and never paid much attention to it anyway.

819
00:41:18,704 --> 00:41:20,204
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we didn't bother.

820
00:41:22,185 --> 00:41:27,408
[SPEAKER_01]: We'd play ball in the back of the hotel or the back of the home or apartment and eat dinner.

821
00:41:27,428 --> 00:41:29,690
[SPEAKER_02]: What would you do in the evenings or off hours?

822
00:41:29,870 --> 00:41:32,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, once in a while you could see pretty good movies.

823
00:41:32,891 --> 00:41:36,233
[SPEAKER_01]: We used to go to the movies on the road.

824
00:41:36,834 --> 00:41:37,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Card playing?

825
00:41:38,014 --> 00:41:39,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we played a lot of cards.

826
00:41:41,843 --> 00:41:43,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, we played a lot of cards.

827
00:41:44,145 --> 00:41:48,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Long train trips, a lot of... Oh boy, yeah, what are you talking about train trips?

828
00:41:49,069 --> 00:41:51,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Killed some of these guys today on that train trip.

829
00:41:52,192 --> 00:41:56,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Leave Boston at 5 o'clock at night and then don't get to St. Louis until the next night at 5.

830
00:41:58,257 --> 00:41:59,859
[SPEAKER_01]: 6 o'clock, St. Louis time.

831
00:42:00,900 --> 00:42:01,981
[SPEAKER_01]: And no air conditioning.

832
00:42:03,322 --> 00:42:04,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Middle of July.

833
00:42:04,523 --> 00:42:05,684
[SPEAKER_01]: We played cards.

834
00:42:08,009 --> 00:42:09,570
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, what would you do to kill the time?

835
00:42:09,630 --> 00:42:10,630
[SPEAKER_02]: You couldn't play cards?

836
00:42:10,650 --> 00:42:11,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Get on the train.

837
00:42:11,631 --> 00:42:13,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Just sit there, huh?

838
00:42:13,632 --> 00:42:16,013
[SPEAKER_01]: You had your own sleeper, see?

839
00:42:17,254 --> 00:42:18,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you sleep much on those things?

840
00:42:19,095 --> 00:42:20,315
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I slept pretty good on them.

841
00:42:20,856 --> 00:42:21,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Hot as they were?

842
00:42:22,056 --> 00:42:22,396
[SPEAKER_02]: Huh?

843
00:42:22,576 --> 00:42:23,857
[SPEAKER_02]: Pretty hot, wasn't it?

844
00:42:23,877 --> 00:42:25,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, sure, it'd be hot as hell.

845
00:42:25,698 --> 00:42:27,719
[SPEAKER_01]: You'd sleep all right.

846
00:42:27,799 --> 00:42:32,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you get to sleep, there wasn't no hotter on the train than there was sleeping in the hotel in St. Louis.

847
00:42:32,882 --> 00:42:33,802
[SPEAKER_01]: It was 118 degrees in there, 15.

848
00:42:37,244 --> 00:42:47,967
[SPEAKER_01]: We fried an egg on the pavement.

849
00:42:48,227 --> 00:42:58,650
[SPEAKER_02]: A lot of horseplay in that great Philadelphia team, among the players?

850
00:42:58,730 --> 00:43:00,090
[SPEAKER_02]: Are they all pretty serious?

851
00:43:00,130 --> 00:43:01,771
[SPEAKER_02]: Were there any jokesters?

852
00:43:01,951 --> 00:43:04,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, they played a lot of jokes.

853
00:43:04,152 --> 00:43:05,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Jimmy Dykes was a jokester.

854
00:43:06,755 --> 00:43:07,896
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a happy family.

855
00:43:08,677 --> 00:43:10,859
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it was.

856
00:43:12,560 --> 00:43:22,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It was just one big happy family.

857
00:43:22,568 --> 00:43:25,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, we'd start our ball game at three o'clock in the afternoon.

858
00:43:25,811 --> 00:43:27,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Six o'clock, we was eating our dinner.

859
00:43:28,153 --> 00:43:32,857
[SPEAKER_01]: It was an hour and a half, two hours.

860
00:43:35,706 --> 00:43:38,268
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd be back home and eating at home?

861
00:43:38,328 --> 00:43:38,688
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

862
00:43:39,149 --> 00:43:41,510
[SPEAKER_02]: Or did you run an apartment in Philadelphia?

863
00:43:41,670 --> 00:43:43,071
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I ran a home.

864
00:43:43,331 --> 00:43:46,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Just you and your wife or a couple of the players?

865
00:43:46,734 --> 00:43:49,576
[SPEAKER_01]: No, myself and my wife and my two children.

866
00:43:50,776 --> 00:43:52,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the same in Boston.

867
00:43:52,398 --> 00:43:53,758
[SPEAKER_01]: I ran an apartment in Boston.

868
00:43:58,041 --> 00:43:59,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Children, I never took them out of school.

869
00:43:59,602 --> 00:44:02,124
[SPEAKER_01]: I was the only player in Maryland in school.

870
00:44:02,184 --> 00:44:03,245
[SPEAKER_01]: I wouldn't change school.

871
00:44:04,233 --> 00:44:08,916
[SPEAKER_01]: And Mr. would stay with him until the school was out and they'd all come live with me then.

872
00:44:10,217 --> 00:44:10,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

873
00:44:10,497 --> 00:44:16,281
[SPEAKER_01]: My boy passed away about three years, two years ago.

874
00:44:16,482 --> 00:44:18,663
[SPEAKER_01]: He's an engineer for Ford Motor Company.

875
00:44:19,223 --> 00:44:20,745
[SPEAKER_02]: Did he play much ball?

876
00:44:20,765 --> 00:44:22,426
[SPEAKER_02]: He played a little up there in New England.

877
00:44:23,406 --> 00:44:24,347
[SPEAKER_02]: Professionally though?

878
00:44:24,407 --> 00:44:24,947
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

879
00:44:28,110 --> 00:44:29,411
[SPEAKER_02]: How did he happen to come here?

880
00:44:29,871 --> 00:44:31,332
[SPEAKER_02]: Is this the Ford Company?

881
00:44:32,318 --> 00:44:42,684
[SPEAKER_01]: He was, I don't know, he used to work for the Kelly Springfield Tire Company and come out this way with Kelly Tire Sales, Kelly Tire Sales.

882
00:44:43,464 --> 00:44:43,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Dealers.

883
00:44:45,325 --> 00:44:52,269
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he got a job out here, he got a place out here with the Ford Motor Company over in San Diego, Indiana.

884
00:44:52,429 --> 00:45:01,274
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a supervisor of engineering and packaging in Connersville, Indiana.

885
00:45:09,848 --> 00:45:11,129
[SPEAKER_02]: So you came here then?

886
00:45:11,849 --> 00:45:13,330
[SPEAKER_02]: After my missus passed away.

887
00:45:13,930 --> 00:45:14,571
[SPEAKER_02]: When was that?

888
00:45:16,031 --> 00:45:17,952
[SPEAKER_02]: I've been here since 1961.

889
00:45:18,433 --> 00:45:18,833
[SPEAKER_02]: I see.

890
00:45:19,353 --> 00:45:20,394
[SPEAKER_02]: You'd been in Lonokone?

891
00:45:20,414 --> 00:45:20,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

892
00:45:21,514 --> 00:45:23,515
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where you lived after your baseball days?

893
00:45:23,535 --> 00:45:24,976
[SPEAKER_02]: That's where I went backstage.

894
00:45:24,996 --> 00:45:26,397
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you run the bowling alley then?

895
00:45:26,557 --> 00:45:27,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

896
00:45:42,402 --> 00:45:47,748
[SPEAKER_00]: Well...

897
00:45:58,939 --> 00:46:02,603
[SPEAKER_00]: This interview was on Thursday, July 18, 1974.

898
00:46:05,883 --> 00:46:13,670
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to attend a meeting in Delaware at Ohio Western at the Ohio Athletic Conference at 2 o'clock in the afternoon on the problem of women's athletics.

899
00:46:14,772 --> 00:46:20,297
[SPEAKER_00]: So I figured this would be a chance to work in a trip to Norwalk and see Lefty Grove.

900
00:46:20,337 --> 00:46:27,804
[SPEAKER_00]: So I left about quarter to 7 in the morning and got up to Norwalk around 10, 15.

901
00:46:27,864 --> 00:46:30,947
[SPEAKER_00]: I had called him on last Sunday evening.

902
00:46:32,740 --> 00:46:33,880
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, I might be up.

903
00:46:33,920 --> 00:46:36,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said, well, give him a call when I get up there.

904
00:46:36,541 --> 00:46:39,822
[SPEAKER_00]: And he would know better then if he could talk to me well.

905
00:46:40,862 --> 00:46:42,082
[SPEAKER_00]: So I didn't know I was going to have an interview.

906
00:46:42,142 --> 00:46:54,565
[SPEAKER_00]: But I got up there and got into town, as I say, a little bit after 10, stopped into an Allen K restaurant to make the phone call.

907
00:46:55,686 --> 00:46:56,526
[SPEAKER_00]: And he wasn't home.

908
00:46:56,566 --> 00:46:58,426
[SPEAKER_00]: I talked to his daughter-in-law, who was bedridden.

909
00:46:58,486 --> 00:47:00,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, he'd be home by 11.

910
00:47:00,167 --> 00:47:01,147
[SPEAKER_00]: Why didn't I come out then?

911
00:47:01,919 --> 00:47:07,102
[SPEAKER_00]: So that gave me a little time to eat, since I wasn't likely to have much time to eat after the interview on my way down to Delaware.

912
00:47:08,183 --> 00:47:09,384
[SPEAKER_00]: So I ate, and I went out there.

913
00:47:09,404 --> 00:47:14,387
[SPEAKER_00]: I got there around 11, and I had a little trouble finding the place, but not too much.

914
00:47:14,427 --> 00:47:17,269
[SPEAKER_00]: It was on the northeast end of town in Norwalk.

915
00:47:18,750 --> 00:47:24,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And I went up and rang the bell, and the bell didn't work, so I knocked on it.

916
00:47:24,354 --> 00:47:25,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Some guy's painting the house in front.

917
00:47:25,655 --> 00:47:27,616
[SPEAKER_00]: He said the bell didn't work, so I rapped on the door.

918
00:47:28,797 --> 00:47:38,005
[SPEAKER_00]: This old lady, an elderly woman, must have been in her late 50s, who was the housekeeper, came and let me in.

919
00:47:38,065 --> 00:47:40,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And she called Mr. Grove.

920
00:47:41,428 --> 00:47:44,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was down in the basement.

921
00:47:44,611 --> 00:47:50,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a pretty hot day, and he's down in the basement, recreation and watching TV in the dark and gloom there.

922
00:47:51,418 --> 00:47:52,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So he came up pretty soon.

923
00:47:52,719 --> 00:47:55,041
[SPEAKER_00]: He was a great big guy, stood there almost

924
00:47:56,215 --> 00:47:57,475
[SPEAKER_00]: like he was challenging me.

925
00:47:57,495 --> 00:48:04,217
[SPEAKER_00]: He came in the room and stood stiff and stern and said, hello or yes or something like that.

926
00:48:05,158 --> 00:48:07,838
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't make any effort to come over to shake my hand at all.

927
00:48:07,958 --> 00:48:09,299
[SPEAKER_00]: So I made the first move.

928
00:48:09,419 --> 00:48:17,321
[SPEAKER_00]: I smiled ingratiatingly and walked over and introduced myself and shook hands.

929
00:48:17,361 --> 00:48:24,403
[SPEAKER_00]: He then shook hands and I explained to him who I was and he knew and I said what I was there for and he remembered.

930
00:48:25,541 --> 00:48:26,861
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, yack him on down.

931
00:48:26,921 --> 00:48:36,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So I didn't have my tape recorder briefcase, so I went back out and got it and came on down into his basement recreation room, as they say.

932
00:48:36,244 --> 00:48:39,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a darkened room there with a television going.

933
00:48:41,105 --> 00:48:43,065
[SPEAKER_00]: He was just down there watching television, I guess.

934
00:48:43,906 --> 00:48:53,288
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a house in a rather nice, rather newly developed area, a small house, which, as you can tell from the tape, was his son's house.

935
00:48:54,580 --> 00:48:57,902
[SPEAKER_00]: And he moved there to live with them when his wife died in 1961.

936
00:48:58,102 --> 00:49:06,928
[SPEAKER_00]: And the son himself is deceased, and the daughter-in-law is bettered, and I did not meet her.

937
00:49:07,008 --> 00:49:15,393
[SPEAKER_00]: She was in bed, and he made no effort to introduce me, so I didn't press, and I was getting rushed for time and a half.

938
00:49:17,553 --> 00:49:23,675
[SPEAKER_00]: This was Lefty Grove, still a tall, handsome man, about 6'3 or so.

939
00:49:24,655 --> 00:49:27,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Had a little stomach on him, but not too much.

940
00:49:27,716 --> 00:49:34,658
[SPEAKER_00]: He was in that shock of handsome gray hair, was still there, hadn't lost any of his hair.

941
00:49:34,758 --> 00:49:45,801
[SPEAKER_00]: His face is well-shaped, somewhat thin and wrinkled as the years have taken their toll, but he's still a pretty handsome guy.

942
00:49:47,145 --> 00:49:54,391
[SPEAKER_00]: This mighty left arm of his didn't seem very big or heavy or strong.

943
00:49:55,172 --> 00:49:56,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Both arms seemed rather slim.

944
00:49:56,733 --> 00:50:05,080
[SPEAKER_00]: He had some advantage in getting fast pitch and whiplash at that pitch.

945
00:50:06,621 --> 00:50:10,024
[SPEAKER_00]: This was one of the hardest interviews I ever had to do.

946
00:50:10,104 --> 00:50:11,505
[SPEAKER_00]: As you could tell, I was kind of

947
00:50:14,316 --> 00:50:15,577
[SPEAKER_00]: strained all the time.

948
00:50:15,737 --> 00:50:17,297
[SPEAKER_00]: He had a speech impediment.

949
00:50:19,258 --> 00:50:20,559
[SPEAKER_00]: He wasn't very friendly.

950
00:50:21,739 --> 00:50:31,523
[SPEAKER_00]: As I was leaving there, after we all got through, I went out, I asked him for some pictures, so we went out in the back and took a couple of his, of him, unsmilingly.

951
00:50:33,684 --> 00:50:36,265
[SPEAKER_00]: But as we walked away, he began to smile a little bit.

952
00:50:36,285 --> 00:50:37,605
[SPEAKER_00]: I showed my appreciation.

953
00:50:37,625 --> 00:50:40,907
[SPEAKER_00]: How proud I was to meet him and talk with him.

954
00:50:42,649 --> 00:50:44,070
[SPEAKER_00]: He began to warm up a little bit.

955
00:50:44,110 --> 00:50:47,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe if I would see him again, it would be a lot easier.

956
00:50:47,492 --> 00:50:50,494
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't volunteer too much.

957
00:50:50,675 --> 00:50:53,777
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't have too much to say.

958
00:50:54,877 --> 00:50:59,581
[SPEAKER_00]: Some of the great thrills of his lifetime didn't seem to be great thrills at all to him.

959
00:51:01,002 --> 00:51:03,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing seemed to excite him very much.

960
00:51:03,103 --> 00:51:05,265
[SPEAKER_00]: He took things pretty much in his stride.

961
00:51:05,285 --> 00:51:11,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Without any sense of humor,

962
00:51:15,395 --> 00:51:24,960
[SPEAKER_00]: So this was not an easy interview.

963
00:51:25,020 --> 00:51:27,081
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose I felt the pressure of time a little bit.

964
00:51:27,121 --> 00:51:28,402
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't get going at 11 o'clock.

965
00:51:28,442 --> 00:51:35,806
[SPEAKER_00]: I had to be in Delaware at 2, and I was wondering if I was going to make it, and I didn't know just how long it would take to get down there.

966
00:51:36,206 --> 00:51:37,887
[SPEAKER_00]: As it turned out, I got there a little bit late.

967
00:51:37,907 --> 00:51:38,928
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't too bad, though.

968
00:51:40,260 --> 00:51:44,584
[SPEAKER_00]: But I could see right away that it was going to be a much shorter interview than I thought.

969
00:51:44,644 --> 00:51:46,246
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be an hour and a half in the hour.

970
00:51:46,266 --> 00:51:53,792
[SPEAKER_00]: But I figured if I got 45 minutes, one side of the tape would be a kind of remarkable thing.

971
00:51:53,812 --> 00:51:55,574
[SPEAKER_00]: The lady just didn't have much to say.

972
00:51:56,855 --> 00:52:04,342
[SPEAKER_00]: And although I had his record right there, I had a daguerreotypes.

973
00:52:06,292 --> 00:52:12,537
[SPEAKER_00]: and had Oliver just hit a triple once in the game, Al Oliver.

974
00:52:13,478 --> 00:52:17,842
[SPEAKER_00]: But I had a Gary Otay's record right there, and I had that to work with.

975
00:52:17,942 --> 00:52:29,251
[SPEAKER_00]: But when somebody's like this, doesn't have much to say, doesn't remember any interesting incidents or any unusual anecdotes, it kind of

976
00:52:34,245 --> 00:52:38,428
[SPEAKER_00]: It slows down your question-developing process.

977
00:52:38,749 --> 00:52:44,273
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought I'd be able to ask plenty of questions, but I don't know.

978
00:52:44,313 --> 00:52:46,054
[SPEAKER_00]: I seemed to run out of gas there.

979
00:52:48,155 --> 00:52:52,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Good leading questions, which normally would open things up, just didn't get anywhere.

980
00:52:52,178 --> 00:52:53,259
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't open up at all.

981
00:52:55,080 --> 00:53:02,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So it was a little disappointing, but I did have a 45-minute interview, and I spent about an hour with them.

982
00:53:04,018 --> 00:53:07,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And I figure I got out of him perhaps as much as I could have.

983
00:53:08,121 --> 00:53:09,202
[SPEAKER_00]: And I did talk to him.

984
00:53:10,262 --> 00:53:11,824
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry I didn't get more, but I did get something.

985
00:53:11,904 --> 00:53:13,445
[SPEAKER_00]: I learned something about the man.

986
00:53:15,406 --> 00:53:18,608
[SPEAKER_00]: And I suppose that made it worthwhile.

987
00:53:20,109 --> 00:53:25,253
[SPEAKER_00]: We went out afterwards in the back, and as I say, I took a couple pictures of him in his T-shirt.

988
00:53:29,630 --> 00:53:32,972
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't exactly a soiled t-shirt, but it wasn't a clean one either.

989
00:53:33,172 --> 00:53:35,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And a pair of kind of work pants on.

990
00:53:37,054 --> 00:53:39,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And I took a couple pictures under the tree.

991
00:53:41,877 --> 00:53:43,958
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I bid goodbye and left.

992
00:53:44,018 --> 00:53:45,959
[SPEAKER_00]: It was about noon or shortly thereafter.

993
00:53:49,501 --> 00:53:56,025
[SPEAKER_00]: I got down a block away off of Parsons on to St. Mary Street and was heading back into town.

994
00:53:57,078 --> 00:54:02,282
[SPEAKER_00]: And I remembered I had a couple of pictures I'd taken along from the autograph, and I'd forgotten to get them out of there.

995
00:54:03,803 --> 00:54:09,728
[SPEAKER_00]: He so quashed my enthusiasm, I didn't even think of that.

996
00:54:10,689 --> 00:54:11,269
[SPEAKER_00]: But I did.

997
00:54:11,369 --> 00:54:12,230
[SPEAKER_00]: I went back then.

998
00:54:12,270 --> 00:54:13,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought I'd better get the autographs.

999
00:54:13,871 --> 00:54:14,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So I got the pictures.

1000
00:54:14,992 --> 00:54:17,794
[SPEAKER_00]: I went, parked, went up the door, and rang the bell.

1001
00:54:17,874 --> 00:54:18,675
[SPEAKER_00]: It didn't ring again.

1002
00:54:19,035 --> 00:54:21,157
[SPEAKER_00]: I panicked, said it didn't ring, and I knocked on the door.

1003
00:54:22,335 --> 00:54:27,116
[SPEAKER_00]: house mother came along and she sent me down where he was watching TV in the dark then.

1004
00:54:29,076 --> 00:54:41,819
[SPEAKER_00]: So I forgot the autograph, so he was very nice about it, signed two pictures I had and gave me a Hall of Fame little card which he had autographed, much like the one, in fact, identical with the one Stanley Kowalewski gave me.

1005
00:54:45,119 --> 00:54:48,260
[SPEAKER_00]: And I thanked him again and took off.

1006
00:54:50,124 --> 00:54:51,025
[SPEAKER_00]: And he wished me well.

1007
00:54:51,045 --> 00:54:55,408
[SPEAKER_00]: He was, I suppose, quite nice, as nice as he is to anybody.

1008
00:54:56,329 --> 00:55:03,134
[SPEAKER_00]: But I was just sorry I didn't have more on that tape.

1009
00:55:05,755 --> 00:55:15,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, to follow me the rest of the day, I left Norwalk about quarter after twelve, then, after the second visit to the house.

1010
00:55:16,748 --> 00:55:20,191
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was getting awfully hot, and the air conditioning in the car didn't work very well.

1011
00:55:21,131 --> 00:55:25,234
[SPEAKER_00]: A little bit worse, I suppose, without it, but it was pretty hot in that car.

1012
00:55:25,254 --> 00:55:32,499
[SPEAKER_00]: The sun was beating right down on me through the windows, and when the air conditioner's on, the sun's on, and it nullifies what the air conditioning does.

1013
00:55:33,400 --> 00:55:45,909
[SPEAKER_00]: I took Route 61, was it, out south of Norwalk, which went for a while, then ran into 598, and I went through Galleon,

1014
00:55:47,745 --> 00:55:51,766
[SPEAKER_00]: And then I hit another route there, 309, took me over to Marion.

1015
00:55:51,826 --> 00:55:54,466
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't have to go through these towns, there were bypasses.

1016
00:55:55,346 --> 00:56:08,669
[SPEAKER_00]: I hit Route 23 outside Marion and got down to Delaware, and went right to the university, and I found the Smith Hall, which is a co-educational dormitory where we had our meeting.

1017
00:56:09,449 --> 00:56:12,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And I got there about a little after 2.

1018
00:56:13,229 --> 00:56:16,990
[SPEAKER_00]: I freshened up, I was hot and perspiring, and I found I'd burned a hole in my

1019
00:56:19,318 --> 00:56:20,259
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess I did that later.

1020
00:56:20,279 --> 00:56:22,841
[SPEAKER_00]: I burned a hole in a nice new shirt I was wearing.

1021
00:56:22,941 --> 00:56:24,303
[SPEAKER_00]: A cigar actually was cut down.

1022
00:56:25,243 --> 00:56:30,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyhow, it was kind of decrepit, so I washed up and freshened up and went to a meeting there.

1023
00:56:31,709 --> 00:56:38,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And this was the special meeting on the status of women's intercollegiate athletics in the Ohio Conference.

1024
00:56:43,163 --> 00:56:53,909
[SPEAKER_00]: We met for about two hours in session and some socializing and other informal business afterwards.

1025
00:56:53,989 --> 00:57:08,157
[SPEAKER_00]: The main thing we did was to repeal the mail rule in the Ohio Conference bylaws, which says only bona fide mail undergraduate students may participate in sports denied sports control by the Ohio Conference.

1026
00:57:09,324 --> 00:57:14,648
[SPEAKER_00]: The Congress has been under heavy pressure from various places, particularly Oberon College, to get rid of this rule.

1027
00:57:15,629 --> 00:57:19,372
[SPEAKER_00]: And threatened by a lawsuit now, they decided to take the pressure.

1028
00:57:19,412 --> 00:57:36,907
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, Oberon is in violation of the rule and was... Oliver...

1029
00:57:37,435 --> 00:57:45,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I was just caught in a rundown when he went home and was finally declared out for falling down and scrambling outside of the baseline.

1030
00:57:47,901 --> 00:57:49,502
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, we repealed the mail.

1031
00:57:49,583 --> 00:57:58,448
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems that Oberlin, who was in trouble with us for violating the mail rule and running Women in Exhibition track meet, was threatening to do this again this fall.

1032
00:57:58,628 --> 00:58:06,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And so some Oberlin students, if we had kept the evidence, replay that now,

1033
00:58:08,246 --> 00:58:08,826
[SPEAKER_00]: If, uh...

1034
00:58:12,407 --> 00:58:14,327
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if he declared him out for any other baseline or anything.

1035
00:58:14,347 --> 00:58:16,628
[SPEAKER_00]: He just stopped running.

1036
00:58:16,688 --> 00:58:21,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't look like the Rumpire made any ruling on that replay.

1037
00:58:23,430 --> 00:58:32,832
[SPEAKER_00]: The group of Oberlin students are going to sue the Ohio Atlantic Conference if they did not permit the women to run an exhibition track this far across country.

1038
00:58:34,092 --> 00:58:37,573
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is what provoked the special meeting.

1039
00:58:38,590 --> 00:58:46,218
[SPEAKER_00]: to either repeal the rule or to collect fund to fight any lawsuit in court.

1040
00:58:48,940 --> 00:58:58,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, I've always felt this rule was, as long as I've been in this rule, good or bad, it had to go.

1041
00:58:58,450 --> 00:59:00,812
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have to go sooner or later, and the sooner the better.

1042
00:59:02,261 --> 00:59:05,504
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're just going to make trouble for yourself, you might as well get rid of it right now.

1043
00:59:05,524 --> 00:59:14,132
[SPEAKER_00]: There are a lot of arguments against it, saying it was going to dilute or wreck women's athletics, because if the women would go out for the men's team now, there wouldn't be any women.

1044
00:59:14,212 --> 00:59:18,516
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have a bad effect on the women's program.

1045
00:59:18,536 --> 00:59:23,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So there were quite a few arguments against it.

1046
00:59:23,941 --> 00:59:26,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Worcester was one of the strongest opponents, and a few other people.

1047
00:59:30,000 --> 00:59:31,441
[SPEAKER_00]: They kicked it around for quite a while.

1048
00:59:31,561 --> 00:59:37,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Finally, they brought it to a vote, and 26 to 1 voted for repeal of the Mayo rule.

1049
00:59:38,986 --> 00:59:39,566
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's out.

1050
00:59:40,647 --> 00:59:51,053
[SPEAKER_00]: The next thing to do was to create a committee to study the whole question of women's intercollegiate athletics in the Ohio Commons.

1051
00:59:51,173 --> 00:59:55,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And in conjunction with some of the women athletic directors, draw up some long-range plan

1052
00:59:56,775 --> 01:00:09,221
[SPEAKER_00]: deciding what sports would be suitable for co-educational participation and what ones would not, and make others study other matters, study Title IX, how this would apply, and so on.

1053
01:00:10,541 --> 01:00:20,426
[SPEAKER_00]: So Everett Bush, the president of the conference, then selected four people, two athletic directors and two faculty representatives to form this committee.

1054
01:00:20,446 --> 01:00:25,428
[SPEAKER_00]: He picked on Ed Sherman and Bob Strymer as the athletic directors, and George Andrews of Oberlin,

1055
01:00:27,203 --> 01:00:29,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Herman Langholz of Capital as the faculty people.

1056
01:00:30,946 --> 01:00:36,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Langholz demurred because he was recently linked to a new legislative committee out in the county.

1057
01:00:36,290 --> 01:00:37,632
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't think he'd have time for it.

1058
01:00:38,452 --> 01:00:43,336
[SPEAKER_00]: So Everett Bush allowed him to get out, and then said he would name somebody later.

1059
01:00:43,356 --> 01:00:51,923
[SPEAKER_00]: We then adjourned, and David Jennings of Ohio Western came over, and we chatted for almost been a half an hour.

1060
01:00:51,943 --> 01:00:56,407
[SPEAKER_00]: He's telling me he's writing this volume on 20th century Ohio history.

1061
01:00:57,170 --> 01:01:01,293
[SPEAKER_00]: commissioned by the Howard Historical Society, and he's got $15,000 to do it.

1062
01:01:02,214 --> 01:01:09,440
[SPEAKER_00]: He's been working on it a couple of years, and he's had my Thom L. Johnson manuscript for a year or so, using that as one of his sources.

1063
01:01:09,480 --> 01:01:17,206
[SPEAKER_00]: And he spent about a half an hour telling me how great it was, how the Johnson manuscript, which he's read four or five times now, is so

1064
01:01:19,640 --> 01:01:21,342
[SPEAKER_00]: The research is so exhaustive.

1065
01:01:21,422 --> 01:01:25,945
[SPEAKER_00]: The interpretation of Johnson is so sound and valuable.

1066
01:01:26,025 --> 01:01:28,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And the methodology is so good.

1067
01:01:28,848 --> 01:01:37,174
[SPEAKER_00]: I gather all the data and I draw conclusions of my own, but I leave it open to the reader to draw his own conclusions.

1068
01:01:37,214 --> 01:01:41,698
[SPEAKER_00]: I give all the evidence there as to draw an object conclusion from what I draw.

1069
01:01:41,978 --> 01:01:48,023
[SPEAKER_00]: He's quite impressed by my fairness and objectivity in my research.

1070
01:01:49,607 --> 01:01:52,608
[SPEAKER_00]: I was really quite embarrassed that he thought it was so good.

1071
01:01:53,228 --> 01:02:01,150
[SPEAKER_00]: And he was borrowing large chunks of it, and he was really afraid that he might be borrowing so much that I'd think he was stealing it.

1072
01:02:01,170 --> 01:02:04,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And the book came out, and he saw all this stuff of mine in there.

1073
01:02:04,210 --> 01:02:04,810
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd get mad.

1074
01:02:05,010 --> 01:02:05,770
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd get mad at him.

1075
01:02:06,691 --> 01:02:10,852
[SPEAKER_00]: So he wanted to get assurance that I would not be mad.

1076
01:02:10,932 --> 01:02:12,052
[SPEAKER_00]: So I told him I wouldn't be mad.

1077
01:02:12,072 --> 01:02:14,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was, in fact, quite glad that I was able to help him.

1078
01:02:14,812 --> 01:02:17,933
[SPEAKER_00]: And the book was that, or the manuscript, that useful.

1079
01:02:18,720 --> 01:02:24,442
[SPEAKER_00]: and really flattered that he found it so sound, scholarly-wise.

1080
01:02:25,983 --> 01:02:27,163
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I was getting quite excited.

1081
01:02:27,183 --> 01:02:29,264
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I may have to get it out and read it over again myself.

1082
01:02:29,344 --> 01:02:30,244
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds so good.

1083
01:02:32,745 --> 01:02:38,207
[SPEAKER_00]: While we were talking, he was telling me how great the thesis was on Tom Johnson, what happened.

1084
01:02:38,227 --> 01:02:43,929
[SPEAKER_00]: But Everett Bush comes up and sits down and interrupts us and said, say, Gene, how would you like to serve on that committee?

1085
01:02:46,472 --> 01:02:53,495
[SPEAKER_00]: the long-range planning committee to work with the women on developing a policy towards women's intercollegiate athletics.

1086
01:02:54,355 --> 01:02:56,676
[SPEAKER_00]: I've never been on any committee in that conference.

1087
01:02:56,776 --> 01:03:04,580
[SPEAKER_00]: None of the standing committees I've ever been questioned or asked, or no one has shown the slightest interest in putting me in any committee.

1088
01:03:04,660 --> 01:03:09,942
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the last thing I ever thought they'd have me in one of the probably more important committees I've ever had.

1089
01:03:10,822 --> 01:03:12,403
[SPEAKER_00]: I was quite taken aback by it.

1090
01:03:13,933 --> 01:03:14,794
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't really know.

1091
01:03:14,914 --> 01:03:17,397
[SPEAKER_00]: I told them I hadn't had much service in the conference.

1092
01:03:17,437 --> 01:03:24,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't think I had enough of a grasp of what... Well, I didn't say that.

1093
01:03:24,245 --> 01:03:26,006
[SPEAKER_00]: I just said my service was rather limited.

1094
01:03:26,787 --> 01:03:31,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, I could do as good a job as anybody else, and probably better, since I have studied the thing maybe more than some.

1095
01:03:35,649 --> 01:03:44,333
[SPEAKER_00]: I was quite pleased to get the chance to do this, so I made a modest disavowal, but he pressed me, so I'm on the committee.

1096
01:03:46,294 --> 01:03:47,175
[SPEAKER_00]: You know about that.

1097
01:03:49,536 --> 01:03:50,537
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm kind of looking forward to this.

1098
01:03:50,577 --> 01:03:54,218
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know when we'll meet and what we'll do, but there's no timetable.

1099
01:03:54,238 --> 01:04:00,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Since we've repealed the rule now, there's no real timetable that we have to meet this fall.

1100
01:04:04,357 --> 01:04:06,477
[SPEAKER_00]: Jenny's went on and finally we broke up.

1101
01:04:06,497 --> 01:04:11,138
[SPEAKER_00]: He took me over to the library there and showed me where he does his research and showed me around the library.

1102
01:04:11,158 --> 01:04:18,960
[SPEAKER_00]: There was a nice audio-visual aid room, a laboratory almost, and a lovely television studio.

1103
01:04:19,200 --> 01:04:25,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Bernie Russey would drool if he saw it, and maybe he has drooled, but he has seen it.

1104
01:04:25,781 --> 01:04:31,122
[SPEAKER_00]: New cameras and a real good lighting system at those, at, uh,

1105
01:04:34,196 --> 01:04:43,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And whenever you call it down in the Basin of Andrews, they're on those network of wires and lights hooked down jerry-can fashion all over the place.

1106
01:04:45,964 --> 01:05:00,714
[SPEAKER_00]: So we looked around there, and about, oh, 5 or 4.30 or so, I left, and we departed, and I went to Bun's restaurant down where I had a hamburger and some french fries.

1107
01:05:02,692 --> 01:05:03,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Then I left for Newark.

1108
01:05:05,274 --> 01:05:07,896
[SPEAKER_00]: The plan was to see Woody English in Newark.

1109
01:05:09,397 --> 01:05:13,861
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd called him last March for an interview and he begged off because of his arthritis.

1110
01:05:15,242 --> 01:05:16,743
[SPEAKER_00]: He said maybe I'd come again this summer.

1111
01:05:16,783 --> 01:05:19,486
[SPEAKER_00]: But we decided I'd just go, Rita and I.

1112
01:05:19,886 --> 01:05:23,849
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be good if I just went up there and barged in.

1113
01:05:25,050 --> 01:05:26,211
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't have a chance to say no.

1114
01:05:27,352 --> 01:05:29,594
[SPEAKER_00]: And maybe he'd talk to me if I was there and saw him.

1115
01:05:31,426 --> 01:05:32,887
[SPEAKER_00]: what I was like and what I was...

1116
01:05:33,007 --> 01:05:35,049
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a chance to explain what I was after.

1117
01:05:36,890 --> 01:05:47,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So I got in Newark around a quarter after six or so, wandered around, got lost a few times, but I only had to ask once or twice to find where North 11th Street was.

1118
01:05:48,420 --> 01:05:49,801
[SPEAKER_00]: I found it and went the wrong way.

1119
01:05:49,821 --> 01:05:51,462
[SPEAKER_00]: I went into a nice residential area.

1120
01:05:51,502 --> 01:05:54,945
[SPEAKER_00]: The other directions looked pretty bad, looked like kind of a downtown.

1121
01:06:04,461 --> 01:06:10,162
[SPEAKER_00]: congested, dirty area, so I didn't think they'd be down there, so I went the other way, but it turned out I should have gone the first way.

1122
01:06:10,182 --> 01:06:17,804
[SPEAKER_00]: They were down in this congested, dirty area, and kind of a crummy little house right next to a bar.

1123
01:06:18,284 --> 01:06:20,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a commercial area around there.

1124
01:06:20,024 --> 01:06:23,685
[SPEAKER_00]: A few homes, old homes, not very well kept up.

1125
01:06:24,765 --> 01:06:30,186
[SPEAKER_00]: Heavy traffic, motorcycles and cars racing around in a terrific racket.

1126
01:06:31,245 --> 01:06:38,027
[SPEAKER_00]: But I found the house and went up there, and there was a little old lady sitting in the front porch, rocking away, nice looking old lady.

1127
01:06:38,047 --> 01:06:42,089
[SPEAKER_00]: My God, could that be Woody English's mother?

1128
01:06:42,709 --> 01:06:47,290
[SPEAKER_00]: I talked to some lady on the phone when I talked to him, and later I talked to him, and he said it was his mother.

1129
01:06:47,390 --> 01:06:48,791
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't believe it would be his mother.

1130
01:06:49,151 --> 01:06:50,091
[SPEAKER_00]: He's 67.

1131
01:06:52,252 --> 01:07:00,215
[SPEAKER_00]: It was Woody English's mother, and there she was, 86 years old, as bright, cheerful, thin as a rail.

1132
01:07:02,763 --> 01:07:08,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Something emaciating, but pleasant, articulate, new baseball.

1133
01:07:08,726 --> 01:07:11,567
[SPEAKER_00]: And I had a delightful half-hour chat with Mrs. English.

1134
01:07:12,608 --> 01:07:18,991
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, I wanted to go out and get the tape recorder and interview her, because Woody wasn't there.

1135
01:07:19,031 --> 01:07:23,733
[SPEAKER_00]: He was away in business, a state farm business, or going to the church or someplace.

1136
01:07:25,315 --> 01:07:29,738
[SPEAKER_00]: She thought he'd be back, but he wasn't, so I waited around a while for him, and I wanted to interview her.

1137
01:07:29,778 --> 01:07:32,699
[SPEAKER_00]: She laughed at that, but no, she didn't want to do that.

1138
01:07:32,739 --> 01:07:33,740
[SPEAKER_00]: It might upset Woody.

1139
01:07:34,420 --> 01:07:35,381
[SPEAKER_00]: He was very careful.

1140
01:07:35,881 --> 01:07:38,923
[SPEAKER_00]: She wouldn't even let me take her picture because he might not like that.

1141
01:07:41,965 --> 01:07:44,006
[SPEAKER_00]: He was really delightful.

1142
01:07:44,086 --> 01:07:45,587
[SPEAKER_00]: She's 86 years old.

1143
01:07:47,848 --> 01:07:49,529
[SPEAKER_00]: She told me a little bit about Woody English.

1144
01:07:50,230 --> 01:07:52,291
[SPEAKER_00]: His arthritis is terribly bad, I guess.

1145
01:07:53,497 --> 01:07:54,957
[SPEAKER_00]: so bad this last winter.

1146
01:07:55,077 --> 01:07:59,118
[SPEAKER_00]: His fingers and his shoulders were so bad and so he couldn't put his shirts on.

1147
01:07:59,158 --> 01:08:00,299
[SPEAKER_00]: She had to help him with his shirts.

1148
01:08:01,159 --> 01:08:03,059
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, I wasn't feeling so well either this winter.

1149
01:08:03,179 --> 01:08:08,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So the two of them are mother and son, and none in very good health.

1150
01:08:10,241 --> 01:08:13,081
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked her if he had been married, because I knew he was married.

1151
01:08:13,121 --> 01:08:15,642
[SPEAKER_00]: It said so in the baseball daguerreotypes.

1152
01:08:16,762 --> 01:08:18,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, he was married yesterday.

1153
01:08:18,283 --> 01:08:19,023
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not now.

1154
01:08:19,624 --> 01:08:22,345
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't offer anymore, so I didn't press anymore.

1155
01:08:22,365 --> 01:08:24,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So apparently, he's either divorced.

1156
01:08:25,147 --> 01:08:26,347
[SPEAKER_00]: I presume that's what it is.

1157
01:08:28,348 --> 01:08:30,009
[SPEAKER_00]: But she knew his baseball career.

1158
01:08:30,029 --> 01:08:34,011
[SPEAKER_00]: She followed it with great interest and knew a lot about it.

1159
01:08:34,051 --> 01:08:39,915
[SPEAKER_00]: She told me how he broke into baseball or how he got into the big time.

1160
01:08:40,915 --> 01:08:44,657
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd been playing locally here and some, I guess, a scout for the Giants.

1161
01:08:48,472 --> 01:08:55,057
[SPEAKER_00]: I didn't get it exactly, whether he scouted him in Newark or saw him play in Columbus and signed him to a contract.

1162
01:08:56,638 --> 01:09:00,021
[SPEAKER_00]: He's 17 years old now, must have been dying in 1924 or so.

1163
01:09:01,342 --> 01:09:05,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Mrs. English's brother, her husband died in 1918, that's one of the

1164
01:09:07,308 --> 01:09:18,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Sad story, she had to tell that her husband, who was a great baseball fan, knew everything about baseball, never lived to see his son make it big in the major leagues.

1165
01:09:20,036 --> 01:09:24,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Then she did have a brother, a man named Paul Cartner.

1166
01:09:24,158 --> 01:09:25,539
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll have to check up on him.

1167
01:09:25,599 --> 01:09:29,741
[SPEAKER_00]: He said he was with the Giants or signed with them or something.

1168
01:09:29,761 --> 01:09:31,302
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if he ever played or not.

1169
01:09:32,961 --> 01:09:37,443
[SPEAKER_00]: And she called up Paul Carpenter to see what he thought about all this.

1170
01:09:37,583 --> 01:09:39,444
[SPEAKER_00]: And he didn't like it much either.

1171
01:09:39,584 --> 01:09:40,885
[SPEAKER_00]: He thought, boy, he was too young.

1172
01:09:41,765 --> 01:09:47,528
[SPEAKER_00]: She finally called the guy, the scout in Columbus, and said, you know how old that boy is?

1173
01:09:48,448 --> 01:09:50,749
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, no, he's 17 years old.

1174
01:09:51,330 --> 01:09:52,970
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can't sign him up.

1175
01:09:53,090 --> 01:09:53,851
[SPEAKER_00]: I want him back.

1176
01:09:53,891 --> 01:09:55,171
[SPEAKER_00]: I want you to give him his release.

1177
01:09:57,241 --> 01:09:58,842
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, oh, we can't do that.

1178
01:09:58,882 --> 01:10:00,182
[SPEAKER_00]: We signed him up.

1179
01:10:00,202 --> 01:10:01,963
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, oh, yes, you can.

1180
01:10:01,983 --> 01:10:03,263
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm controlling this.

1181
01:10:03,363 --> 01:10:04,683
[SPEAKER_00]: I want you to give him his release.

1182
01:10:04,723 --> 01:10:06,044
[SPEAKER_00]: I want you to give it right now.

1183
01:10:08,305 --> 01:10:10,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really funny hearing her talk so emphatically.

1184
01:10:10,665 --> 01:10:12,166
[SPEAKER_00]: She chuckles along with it.

1185
01:10:13,006 --> 01:10:19,568
[SPEAKER_00]: The impressive stance she took.

1186
01:10:21,829 --> 01:10:24,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So he gave him his release when he came back home.

1187
01:10:24,828 --> 01:10:32,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Then later on, her brother, Paul Cartner, said, well, he'll have plenty of offers and good ones too, so don't worry about that.

1188
01:10:32,811 --> 01:10:33,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's get over it.

1189
01:10:34,072 --> 01:10:37,813
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it wasn't long, though, before a Toledo scout apparently came down and got it.

1190
01:10:37,953 --> 01:10:40,714
[SPEAKER_00]: And he signed him, and this was all right with her.

1191
01:10:40,734 --> 01:10:48,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe it was a year later, and he was older now, and she decided to reconcile herself with his playing professional baseball.

1192
01:10:51,970 --> 01:10:55,873
[SPEAKER_00]: So they'd signed it, and then she said, well, doesn't he get anything out of this?

1193
01:10:55,933 --> 01:11:00,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Meaning, your son, Woody, doesn't he get anything from this contract?

1194
01:11:01,877 --> 01:11:03,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you better give him something.

1195
01:11:03,298 --> 01:11:05,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So she slapped my knee and chuckled.

1196
01:11:05,800 --> 01:11:07,121
[SPEAKER_00]: She said, you know, they gave him $500.

1197
01:11:07,381 --> 01:11:11,744
[SPEAKER_00]: And she laughed, all because of her pressing the matter.

1198
01:11:11,764 --> 01:11:12,625
[SPEAKER_00]: She got $500.

1199
01:11:12,865 --> 01:11:17,088
[SPEAKER_00]: As we said, it wouldn't sound like much today, but it was a great thing.

1200
01:11:17,782 --> 01:11:21,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And then after a short time at Toledo, I guess, the Cubs learned about him.

1201
01:11:21,705 --> 01:11:28,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And they paid Toledo $50,000 plus two players for Woody English.

1202
01:11:31,314 --> 01:11:34,556
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, she found his career very closely.

1203
01:11:34,616 --> 01:11:38,960
[SPEAKER_00]: She saw a couple World Series, I guess three of them.

1204
01:11:39,100 --> 01:11:40,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Those were all the World Series.

1205
01:11:42,085 --> 01:11:46,788
[SPEAKER_00]: He lived in Chicago permanently when I went up there, year-round residence there.

1206
01:11:46,828 --> 01:11:50,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And he only came back on visits to Newark.

1207
01:11:51,250 --> 01:11:52,611
[SPEAKER_00]: He was born and raised in Newark.

1208
01:11:52,871 --> 01:11:56,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I guess he may have been born out in Alexandria a short distance away.

1209
01:11:56,533 --> 01:12:00,175
[SPEAKER_00]: But he lived in Newark at an early time.

1210
01:12:01,616 --> 01:12:04,597
[SPEAKER_00]: And I guess I didn't get too much on his career there.

1211
01:12:04,817 --> 01:12:07,639
[SPEAKER_00]: If I really had to get going, I'd drive back to Marietta.

1212
01:12:09,165 --> 01:12:18,168
[SPEAKER_00]: I couldn't really do all I wanted to do, but I did find out a few of the bare facts.

1213
01:12:19,568 --> 01:12:31,752
[SPEAKER_00]: He said he was just a frail kid when he broke in, and the Toledo scout said he looked like a piece of soap which had been

1214
01:12:32,686 --> 01:12:35,146
[SPEAKER_00]: He was in the washing all day long, something like that.

1215
01:12:35,186 --> 01:12:37,447
[SPEAKER_00]: He laughed at that, whatever the figure of speech was.

1216
01:12:38,027 --> 01:12:43,348
[SPEAKER_00]: He was so, well, apparently small and frail at that age when he broke in.

1217
01:12:43,628 --> 01:12:50,569
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how big he ever got, but he was pretty shriveled, I guess.

1218
01:12:52,290 --> 01:12:56,370
[SPEAKER_00]: They laughed at the figure of speech he was describing.

1219
01:13:05,721 --> 01:13:09,523
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't come back to Newark until 1959.

1220
01:13:09,903 --> 01:13:16,086
[SPEAKER_00]: He was with, I don't know what he did after his plane crew in Chicago.

1221
01:13:17,087 --> 01:13:23,930
[SPEAKER_00]: He came back to Newark and got a job with State Farm Insurance, I think it was, and worked with them for a number of years.

1222
01:13:23,990 --> 01:13:28,412
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said he retired when he was 66 or so.

1223
01:13:28,492 --> 01:13:32,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that would have been just last year, as I have it.

1224
01:13:32,274 --> 01:13:32,954
[SPEAKER_00]: He was born in 1907.

1225
01:13:35,685 --> 01:13:40,968
[SPEAKER_00]: And he has been retired for some time, so maybe it was in 66 he retired.

1226
01:13:41,908 --> 01:13:45,330
[SPEAKER_00]: But these are the things I'll have to find out when I get to interviewing him.

1227
01:13:46,210 --> 01:13:48,991
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he's lived there with her.

1228
01:13:52,133 --> 01:13:53,253
[SPEAKER_00]: They didn't always live in this house.

1229
01:13:53,273 --> 01:13:54,614
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know how long they lived in this house.

1230
01:13:54,634 --> 01:13:55,654
[SPEAKER_00]: They lived someplace else.

1231
01:13:55,694 --> 01:13:57,795
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's lived with her since he came back.

1232
01:13:58,455 --> 01:13:59,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe there was something.

1233
01:13:59,556 --> 01:14:02,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe his marriage broke up at this time he came back home.

1234
01:14:03,078 --> 01:14:03,578
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

1235
01:14:05,545 --> 01:14:08,067
[SPEAKER_00]: But he lives here with her now.

1236
01:14:08,687 --> 01:14:12,650
[SPEAKER_00]: There were four children, two boys and two girls.

1237
01:14:12,830 --> 01:14:16,072
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the boys and one of the girls are deceased.

1238
01:14:16,893 --> 01:14:23,577
[SPEAKER_00]: The other daughter is living in Newark and has a job with the county clerk in the county courthouse.

1239
01:14:23,637 --> 01:14:26,860
[SPEAKER_00]: I think she said she was married and they have a couple of children.

1240
01:14:28,601 --> 01:14:31,543
[SPEAKER_00]: At least there's somebody else in the town to look after them.

1241
01:14:36,440 --> 01:14:37,561
[SPEAKER_00]: I wanted to take her picture.

1242
01:14:37,601 --> 01:14:43,027
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it would be great having this little old lady in her front porch of this rather run-down house.

1243
01:14:43,047 --> 01:14:45,830
[SPEAKER_00]: And she didn't want to do that.

1244
01:14:46,630 --> 01:14:52,777
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked her if she had any papers or pictures or scrapbooks or things that he did.

1245
01:14:53,217 --> 01:14:59,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said he brought an awful lot of stuff with him when he came back from Chicago in 1959.

1246
01:15:00,833 --> 01:15:10,198
[SPEAKER_00]: But you know, that very year that he got there in 1959, there was a terrible flood in Newark, and the water in the basement and all this stuff got ruined by the floodwaters.

1247
01:15:10,898 --> 01:15:13,500
[SPEAKER_00]: They were able to salvage a little bit, but not very much.

1248
01:15:13,880 --> 01:15:19,563
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know, they tried drying it up, but they finally, I guess they threw it out.

1249
01:15:19,863 --> 01:15:22,384
[SPEAKER_00]: She didn't say what they did, but it was lost.

1250
01:15:22,444 --> 01:15:25,226
[SPEAKER_00]: So whatever he has, it isn't much.

1251
01:15:25,786 --> 01:15:27,367
[SPEAKER_00]: She was saying that just the other day,

1252
01:15:28,555 --> 01:15:31,738
[SPEAKER_00]: He does get a lot of requests from kids for pictures and autographs.

1253
01:15:31,818 --> 01:15:36,181
[SPEAKER_00]: And just the other day, I was asking her, I said, mother, where are all those old pictures?

1254
01:15:36,922 --> 01:15:38,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they in a box somewhere?

1255
01:15:38,563 --> 01:15:44,508
[SPEAKER_00]: And she said, well, maybe they are around here somewhere, but they haven't seen them lately.

1256
01:15:44,849 --> 01:15:46,570
[SPEAKER_00]: But there may still be something there.

1257
01:15:49,072 --> 01:15:49,853
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked her what?

1258
01:15:52,268 --> 01:15:54,969
[SPEAKER_00]: That would be the best time to try and come and see him.

1259
01:15:55,049 --> 01:16:11,493
[SPEAKER_00]: I just wanted to come and say hello this time so he'd get to know me and be less unwilling to see me for an interview.

1260
01:16:14,015 --> 01:16:23,703
[SPEAKER_00]: met me and realized I was serious and really wanted to talk baseball for a good purpose with him, he might be much more agreeable to doing this.

1261
01:16:23,743 --> 01:16:25,685
[SPEAKER_00]: So that was my purpose of coming there.

1262
01:16:26,286 --> 01:16:32,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I hoped that she would explain to him this, now that she had met me and knew that I was

1263
01:16:35,717 --> 01:16:41,560
[SPEAKER_00]: sincere in my efforts to have a good interview here, and she would ease the way for me.

1264
01:16:41,700 --> 01:16:43,822
[SPEAKER_00]: She said she'd be very happy to do that.

1265
01:16:44,582 --> 01:16:47,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I asked her what would be a good time to come up and talk to him.

1266
01:16:47,123 --> 01:16:48,504
[SPEAKER_00]: She said, well, just about now.

1267
01:16:49,084 --> 01:16:54,788
[SPEAKER_00]: You ought to just pop right in unexpectedly, and then he won't have a chance to say no.

1268
01:16:54,808 --> 01:16:58,230
[SPEAKER_00]: If you call him on the phone, he'll have a chance to say no.

1269
01:16:58,790 --> 01:17:00,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly what Rita had said would be the way to do it.

1270
01:17:05,575 --> 01:17:07,975
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we agreed.

1271
01:17:08,055 --> 01:17:10,516
[SPEAKER_00]: We'd trap him that way, she and I.

1272
01:17:12,176 --> 01:17:14,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So I'll come up about supper time sometime.

1273
01:17:14,597 --> 01:17:22,698
[SPEAKER_00]: And I asked her to advise him that we might be up some evening next week.

1274
01:17:23,718 --> 01:17:25,018
[SPEAKER_00]: And I want to talk to him.

1275
01:17:25,038 --> 01:17:27,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So I think that's what we may do.

1276
01:17:27,939 --> 01:17:29,259
[SPEAKER_00]: Rita will be home next week.

1277
01:17:29,279 --> 01:17:31,899
[SPEAKER_00]: She's been on her visitations all summer with Kathy.

1278
01:17:31,919 --> 01:17:33,940
[SPEAKER_00]: Kathy leaves for New Orleans tomorrow morning.

1279
01:17:33,960 --> 01:17:34,840
[SPEAKER_00]: She's packing out.

1280
01:17:35,529 --> 01:17:37,529
[SPEAKER_00]: They just got back late last night.

1281
01:17:37,669 --> 01:17:43,810
[SPEAKER_00]: I got home about 9 o'clock, and they got back about 11 o'clock from Charleston for another week of visitations.

1282
01:17:43,970 --> 01:17:48,491
[SPEAKER_00]: And we're as a grand worthy advisor of the rainbow this summer.

1283
01:17:48,651 --> 01:17:56,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And she's going this next week to the Supreme Assembly, which is a national rainbow gathering in New Orleans.

1284
01:17:57,153 --> 01:18:03,614
[SPEAKER_00]: They'll be meeting partners there by bus tomorrow morning, get down there Monday maybe, and then come back a week from Sunday.

1285
01:18:04,797 --> 01:18:11,199
[SPEAKER_00]: So Rita will be here, and she and I thought we might take a few trips, maybe go down and see Fred Blake in Beckley.

1286
01:18:12,040 --> 01:18:29,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we can do a little bit of touring, visit this restored village up at Monroeville, or wherever it is, and then maybe restore the village in the Ohio Historical Society grounds, visit the mounds in Newark, and maybe wrap up a day of touring

1287
01:18:30,879 --> 01:18:35,300
[SPEAKER_00]: by going over to Newark around 6 o'clock and dropping in at Woody English.

1288
01:18:40,742 --> 01:18:46,704
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's our plan.

1289
01:18:47,444 --> 01:18:52,806
[SPEAKER_00]: We might also take a trip down to West Virginia, as if she hadn't seen enough of it.

1290
01:18:52,826 --> 01:18:54,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Might go to Grantsville, find out about this.

1291
01:19:00,510 --> 01:19:03,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Guy Pollack and his Seneca books, if he is for real.

1292
01:19:03,271 --> 01:19:10,012
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't heard from him for weeks about the Peck book, which I may be editing if they want to publish it.

1293
01:19:11,092 --> 01:19:14,673
[SPEAKER_00]: And then we'll move on down to Beckley and perhaps a few other things.

1294
01:19:17,273 --> 01:19:19,754
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those are the highlights of that trip.

1295
01:19:19,814 --> 01:19:26,475
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought it'd be a good idea, while they were fresh in my memory, to say something about the Grove interview.

1296
01:19:27,613 --> 01:19:30,295
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good idea to say something about every interview I have.

1297
01:19:30,355 --> 01:19:31,356
[SPEAKER_00]: I should be doing this.

1298
01:19:31,496 --> 01:19:32,557
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't very much.

1299
01:19:33,457 --> 01:19:41,924
[SPEAKER_00]: But at least the Mrs. English interview, because she was really good, and I could have taped her very well if she had been agreeable.

1300
01:19:41,944 --> 01:19:46,087
[SPEAKER_00]: I could have had a good 45 minutes or an hour taped of her.

1301
01:19:46,107 --> 01:19:47,388
[SPEAKER_00]: It would have been very useful.

1302
01:19:50,025 --> 01:19:53,066
[SPEAKER_00]: But I couldn't force that on her.

1303
01:19:53,086 --> 01:20:01,550
[SPEAKER_00]: But maybe if I can get up there, Rita and I can get up there, we can get the two of them together and get a real good hour and a half with the both of them going.

1304
01:20:01,770 --> 01:20:06,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Because she can add an awful lot, as wives have done.

1305
01:20:06,912 --> 01:20:07,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Not mothers.

1306
01:20:07,492 --> 01:20:09,413
[SPEAKER_00]: I've never interviewed anybody's mother before.

1307
01:20:09,433 --> 01:20:12,974
[SPEAKER_00]: A lot of wives, but the same idea.

1308
01:20:14,294 --> 01:20:17,396
[SPEAKER_00]: OK, I haven't quite used up the whole tape.

1309
01:20:17,456 --> 01:20:18,236
[SPEAKER_00]: I see there's about

1310
01:20:19,527 --> 01:20:26,650
[SPEAKER_00]: a third of this side still remaining, but I think I've got all I have to say on this, so I'll be signing off.

1311
01:20:26,710 --> 01:20:30,131
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the day after the interview.

1312
01:20:30,171 --> 01:20:41,395
[SPEAKER_00]: This is Friday, the 19th of July, 1974, as I watched the Pirates of Atlanta ballot out here at 9.15 in the evening.

1313
01:20:42,420 --> 01:20:49,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Now I'm going to sign off and continue writing the chapter on the feud in the Van Johnson story.

1314
01:20:49,807 --> 01:20:53,811
[SPEAKER_00]: About halfway through this chapter on the feud with Comiskey and Johnson.

1315
01:20:54,051 --> 01:20:55,913
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll keep a wick on that now.

1316
01:20:56,093 --> 01:20:58,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So that'll be all for this evening.

